INFO:self_evolverec:The new idea for model in iteration 1:
{
  "description": "Add a bounded recent-history directed-transition feature to frozen per-category SASRec scores, selecting one shared nonnegative fusion coefficient through a small predeclared validation grid.",
  "motivation": "The user diagnosis identifies broad relatedness without a reason to choose now and excessive influence from older incidental interactions. Recent observed transitions provide an explicit continuation signal addressing both, while preserving SASRec's contextual score. The model diagnosis identifies positive–negative confusion, particularly Electronics' 14.95% wrong-ranking rate and negative lower-tail margin; an additional ranking signal is more directly motivated than an embedding-collapse remedy. Transition support remains an imperfect behavioral proxy, not evidence of verified compatibility, unmet need, or complementarity.",
  "implementation_notes": "Train the unchanged RecHarness sasrec_perdataset baseline with preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, existing splits and checkpoint selection, single-GPU execution, all four category routes, and SASREC_EPOCHS environment default 200. Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']; save all artifacts beneath the trial directory. Freeze each baseline checkpoint before fusion experiments and reuse it across all scoring ablations.\n\nFor each category, construct directed counts c_ij from every adjacent pair in training histories, counting repeated events. Exclude padding and reserve item ID 0. Let c_i be the source's total outgoing count before pruning. Retain the 64 largest outgoing counts per source, breaking ties by ascending destination ID. Let N be the number of real items. Record training item frequencies and p_pop(j) = (frequency(j)+1)/(total_frequency+N). Popularity-backed Dirichlet smoothing is p_smooth(j|i) = (c_ij+10*p_pop(j))/(c_i+10). The selected transition feature deliberately uses only its empirical component e_ij = c_ij/(c_i+10), on retained edges, and zero otherwise; it does not claim to equal the full smoothed distribution. This keeps source reliability shrinkage while separating popularity evidence. Missing or unsupported sources have zero transition contribution and fall back to SASRec.\n\nFor each causal prefix, select its latest five nonpadding items. Assign normalized weights exp(-lag)/sum(exp(-valid_lags)), where lag zero is newest; repeated source items accumulate evidence. Define T(prefix,j) as the weighted sum of log1p(100*e_ij)/log(101), so T is in [0,1]. Empty histories give T=0. Combined scores are s_SASRec(prefix,j)+alpha*T(prefix,j). The constants five, 64, 10, and 100 are fixed design choices, not additional searched hyperparameters. Select a single alpha shared across categories from [0,0.25,0.5,1,2], using pinned macro validation HR@10; prefer the smaller coefficient on exact ties. Coefficients are in baseline-logit units, and category logit scales may differ. Diagnose this limitation rather than introducing a category-specific search. No inference-only correction for the baseline negative-sampling distribution is applied.\n\nUse sorted int64 edge keys source*(N+1)+destination, checking for integer overflow, and batched torch.searchsorted for candidate scoring. Guard indices beyond the array length before reading, verify exact key equality, and return zero for misses. Retain CSR rows for full-catalog scoring: scatter the same weighted edge features into a batch-by-catalog buffer, or process users in chunks to control memory. Preserve the baseline's candidate filtering and routing. Verify that candidate scores equal indexed full-catalog scores, including repeated histories, padding, empty histories, unsupported sources, and retained-edge ties. Update the predictor writer to load the checkpoint, graph buffers, constants, and selected shared alpha using identical scoring.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions. view.model must be the actual wrapper nn.Module holding SASRec and transition buffers; expose item_emb and the original compatibility methods. log2feats(sequence) must preserve each position's causal prefix, and predict_score(features, items) must reproduce deployed combined scores. Bind any sequence context explicitly to its returned feature batch rather than relying on an unguarded mutable last-batch cache. Training-position diagnostics must additionally report leave-user-out transition margins: use the supplied sequence to identify matching training histories, conservatively excluding every matching user's complete edge counts, and normalize and prune after subtraction. If unique attribution is unavailable, disclose that limitation and omit the corrected margin rather than claiming independence. Report full-graph margins separately as target-reusing descriptive measurements. Apply identical diagnostic feature construction to every method being compared. Metadata category breakdowns must be omitted or marked unavailable, since item categories are not provided. Never fabricate ratings, reviews, prices, stores, compatibility labels, or simulator traits.\n\nThe first experiment does not learn alpha from training targets, so no cross-fitting is required for fusion fitting. If joint learning is later attempted, use deterministic user-level cross-fitting: construct each user's transition features from other users' folds, excluding all of that user's overlapping prefixes; use the full training graph only at inference. A cheaper event-level subtraction removes the current event from both c_ij and c_i but does not provide the same protection against same-user overlapping-prefix reuse. For exact-zero trainable alpha, use a direct parameter and project it into [0,2] after each optimizer step. A sigmoid initialized near zero is an alternative, but cannot achieve exact zero with a nonzero ordinary derivative at a finite interior parameter. Do not silently replace the frozen experiment with either variant.\n\nPredeclare baseline, popularity-only, transition-only, SASRec+popularity, and SASRec+transition controls. Use P(j)=log1p(100*p_pop(j))/log(101) for the popularity residual and the same coefficient grid, recording ties and coverage. Defer combined popularity-plus-transition fusion to a separately logged experiment. Keep evaluation users, candidates, checkpoint, and host tie handling identical across ablations; do not modify the evaluator. Select solely by the common equal-weight macro validation HR@10. If the host exposes per-user validation hits, perform 10,000 paired bootstrap replicates resampling 200 users within each category, sharing indices across methods; otherwise do not invent uncertainty intervals. Define support strata as zero, positive counts below the median positive training-edge count, and counts at or above it, using the latest source; define popularity bins by training-frequency quartiles. Report subgroup sizes, paired wins/losses, target and candidate transition coverage, and recommended-item popularity where outputs permit. Sparse strata remain descriptive. Bootstrap intervals condition on trained models and fixed candidates and do not remove validation-selection optimism. Log failures and every configuration against the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget, freeze the selected method before independent held-out evaluation, and make no test or full-catalog accuracy claims from sampled validation.",
  "pseudocode": "ALPHAS = [0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0]\nfor category in FOUR_CATEGORIES:\n    sasrec[category] = train_unchanged_baseline(seed=42, epochs=environment_default_200)\n    freeze(sasrec[category])\n    counts = count_adjacent_events(training_histories[category], exclude_padding=True)\n    source_totals = outgoing_totals(counts)  # Before pruning.\n    retained = top64_per_source(counts, tie_break='ascending_item_id')\n    edge_feature = {\n        (i,j): log1p(100 * c_ij / (source_totals[i] + 10)) / log(101)\n        for (i,j), c_ij in retained.items()\n    }\n    save_sorted_keys_values_and_CSR(edge_feature)\n    save_training_popularity_statistics()\n\n# Shared by candidate scoring and full-catalog inference.\ndef transition_score(prefix, candidates, graph):\n    recent = latest_nonpadding(prefix, limit=5)\n    if len(recent) == 0:\n        return zeros_like(candidates, dtype=float)\n    weights = normalize([exp(-lag) for lag in range(len(recent))])\n    return sum(weights[lag] * graph.lookup(source, candidates, default=0)\n               for lag, source in enumerate(reversed(recent)))\n\ndef score(category, prefix, candidates, alpha):\n    return sasrec[category].score(prefix, candidates) + alpha * transition_score(\n        prefix, candidates, graph[category])\n\n# Use the host's unchanged pinned validation protocol.\nfor alpha in ALPHAS:\n    macro_hr = host_validation_macro_hr(score, shared_alpha=alpha)\n    log_attempt(alpha, macro_hr, per_category_results, cost)\nselected_alpha = argmax_macro_hr_with_smaller_alpha_tiebreak()\nrun_predeclared_popularity_and_transition_controls_on_same_checkpoint()\n# No test access; uncertainty/strata only when host validation outputs permit.\n\n# Honest training-position probes need different, target-independent graph statistics.\nfor diagnostic_batch in train_loader:\n    infer_matching_users_from_public_training_sequences()\n    subtract_complete_matching_user_edge_counts()\n    renormalize_and_reprune_for_probe()\n    compute_causal_leave_user_out_margins_or_mark_unavailable()\n\nsave_checkpoints_graphs_constants_and_selected_alpha(trial_directory)\nwrite_predictor_with_identical_scoring_and_four_category_routing()\nverify_candidate_scores_equal_indexed_full_catalog_scores()",
  "originality": {
    "score": 4,
    "positive": "The diagnosis-driven combination of recent continuation evidence, support shrinkage, and explicit popularity controls creates a focused, falsifiable adaptation.",
