INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: At 0% progress, prioritize a small, testable extension: **SASRec with a gated recent-and-repeated-interest residual**, followed by a separate loss ablation. No benchmark improvement has yet been demonstrated.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec can represent sequential preferences, but its objective does not explicitly distinguish recurring interests from incidental interactions. This matches users’ complaints about older, occasional purchases dominating recommendations. (2) Electronics has 14.95% negative-over-positive rankings and a −0.4812 lower-tail margin. These measurements motivate better discrimination, but do not establish the cause or predict validation gains. (3) Embedding similarity scores near 0.54–0.56 provide little evidence of severe collapse. Increasing diversity through strong embedding regularization is therefore a lower priority.

**Insights from related work.** (1) MSMT-LSI separates long- and short-term interests, while HyperHawkes models recurrence. Together they motivate preserving repeated interests without assuming that all older evidence is irrelevant. (2) gSASRec and eSASRec motivate controlled multi-negative loss comparisons. Their findings do not establish that this benchmark needs harder negatives. (3) Graph-projection methods suggest directed transitions can supplement similarity. Such transitions remain behavioral evidence, not verified equipment compatibility.

**Framework.** Organize experiments along three directions: intent representation, ranking supervision, and transition evidence. SASRec supplies the common backbone; gated interest pooling changes representation, sampled softmax changes supervision, and a sparse transition residual changes evidence. Test each independently before combining them.

**Candidate assessments.** Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Each cell gives a benefit and limitation.

| Idea | Originality | Potential | User alignment | Model alignment | Difficulty |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Gated recent/repeated residual | 4: useful combination; established components | 8: adaptable; intent remains latent | 8: targets stale influence and broad matches; cannot verify compatibility | 7: sharper contexts; margin gains uncertain | 3: pooling and gate; causal masking needed |
| 128-negative sampled softmax | 2: established; little novelty | 8: strong baseline; sampling-sensitive | 5: improves discrimination; no explicit intent model | 9: targets ranking confusion; false negatives persist | 3: compact loss; memory overhead |
| Directed transition residual | 5: complementary evidence; known approach | 7: interpretable; sparse transitions | 7: supports plausible next choices; associations can mislead | 6: additional signal; popularity confounding | 5: sparse graph; serving integration |

**Selected algorithm.** Add a small gated residual pooling the last five valid embeddings and historical embeddings supported by recent interests. Similarity-weighted pooling lets multiple supporting interactions accumulate evidence. This explicitly targets **older incidental influence** and **broad relevance crowding out specific recent matches**. It also targets Electronics’ weak separation through more focused contextual features; this remains a hypothesis.

Retain baseline loss initially. Compare baseline, residual-only, and loss-only models using unchanged macro validation HR@10. Track per-category results, GPU cost, and unchanged margin probes. Compatibility and purchase satisfaction remain unobserved; full-catalog usefulness requires separate qualitative evidence.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: At 0% progress, prioritize a small, testable extension: **SASRec with a gated recent-and-supported-history residual**, followed by a separate loss ablation. No benchmark improvement has been demonstrated; recurrence is a hypothesis requiring additional controls.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec attention can reflect repetition, but normalized pooling does not preserve absolute evidence counts. Similarity-weighted history therefore measures contextual support, not established recurring intent. (2) Electronics has 14.95% negative-over-positive rankings and a −0.4812 lower-tail margin. These motivate discrimination experiments without identifying their cause. (3) Embedding similarity near 0.54–0.56 provides little evidence of severe collapse, making strong diversity regularization a lower priority.

**Insights from related work.** (1) MSMT-LSI and SHAN motivate separating recent and historical preferences; HyperHawkes explicitly accumulates temporal triggering evidence. Its recurrence interpretation does not transfer automatically to normalized embedding pooling. (2) gSASRec and eSASRec motivate loss-only controls, with sampling policies documented precisely. (3) Graph projections motivate directed transition evidence, which cannot establish equipment compatibility.

**Framework.** Organize experiments along intent representation, ranking supervision, and transition evidence. SASRec remains the backbone; gated pooling changes representation, sampled softmax changes supervision, and a sparse transition residual changes evidence. Within pooling, distinguish recent evidence, older contextual support, and absolute support counts. Test components independently before combining them.

**Candidate assessments.** Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Each cell gives a benefit and limitation.

| Idea | Originality | Potential | User alignment | Model alignment | Difficulty |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Gated recent/history residual | 3: compact combination; established pooling | 7: useful platform; recurrence unproven | 8: targets stale influence and broad matches; cannot verify compatibility | 7: focused contexts; margin gains uncertain | 4: small modules; masking and serving integration |
| 128-negative sampled softmax | 2: established; little novelty | 8: strong control; sampler-sensitive | 5: discrimination; no explicit intent attribution | 9: targets confusion; false negatives persist | 3: compact loss; memory overhead |
| Directed transition residual | 5: distinct evidence; known approach | 7: interpretable; sparse transitions | 7: plausible next choices; associations mislead | 6: additional signal; popularity confounding | 5: sparse graph; serving integration |

**Selected algorithm.** Retain the original full-history pooling residual, renamed to avoid claiming recurrence. Recent pooling targets **older incidental influence**; recent-conditioned historical pooling targets **broad relevance crowding out specific current matches**. Better contextual discrimination targets Electronics’ weak margins. These are hypotheses, not demonstrated benefits.

Keep baseline loss initially. Compare baseline, residual-only, and loss-only models; prioritize a matched recent-only residual control. Then isolate disjoint older support and compare occurrence versus deduplicated pooling. Hold recent context fixed when removing or duplicating older support; control recency, popularity, parameter count, residual scale, and tuning effort. Only incremental older-history benefit supports a recurrence interpretation.

Select by unchanged macro validation HR@10 against 99 sampled negatives. Track category scores, margins, GPU cost, and all trials. Paired within-category bootstrap intervals are exploratory and conditional on selection; they do not remove winner’s optimism or establish full-catalog superiority.
