INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: At 0% progress, prioritize a small, testable extension: **recency-weighted transition residual scoring with multi-negative training**. All benefits below are hypotheses requiring pinned validation.

**Insights and directions.** Three starting-point insights: (1) Electronics’ 14.95% negative-over-positive rate motivates stronger ranking supervision; sampled training margins do not establish validation HR@10. (2) Embedding similarity near 0.54 does not justify prioritizing collapse remedies. (3) User feedback identifies both incidental-history influence and broadly related recommendations lacking a supported next-step purpose; these motivate recency weighting and directed transitions.

Three literature insights: (1) [GaGNN](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ins.2024.120623) motivates transition evidence alongside sequence representations, without establishing compatibility. (2) [LSIDN](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.14743) motivates distinguishing persistent interests from recent intent; additional encoders increase implementation cost. (3) [eSASRec](https://arxiv.org/html/2508.06450v1) motivates sampled softmax, although its architectural changes confound attribution. These define three directions: transition evidence, temporal relevance, and ranking supervision.

| Framework axis | Existing approach | Proposed role / gap |
|---|---|---|
| Sequence context | SASRec attention | Retain personalized context |
| Recent intent | LSIDN separate encoders | Cheap decay over recent sources |
| Directed association | GaGNN graph modeling | Smoothed training-only transition residual |
| Candidate discrimination | eSASRec sampled softmax | Multiple uniform negatives |

**Candidate assessments.** Scores are originality / future potential / user alignment / model alignment / code difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder. Each cell gives a positive and a limitation.

| Idea | Originality | Potential | User | Model | Difficulty |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Multi-negative SASRec | 2: direct; established | 7: reusable; incremental | 4: finer ranking; no temporal mechanism | 9: targets confusion; false negatives | 2: small change; masking needed |
| Gated recent/history fusion | 4: targeted; familiar | 7: extensible; gate instability | 8: incidental-interest control; no prerequisite evidence | 6: context improves; separation indirect | 5: modular; inference changes |
| **Recent transition residual + sampled softmax** | 4: useful combination; established parts | 8: reusable; sparse edges | 8: recent purpose; compatibility unresolved | 8: stronger discrimination; graph bias | 4: sparse counts; scoring integration |

**Selected algorithm.** Build directed adjacent-item counts exclusively from training histories. Smooth destination probabilities toward training popularity. Aggregate clipped log-lift from recent history items with exponential positional decay; add this residual to SASRec scores. Train using multiple uniform negatives, excluding known training positives and targets.

This explicitly targets older/incidental influence through decay and broad relatedness through observed directional associations. Multi-negative competition targets Electronics’ weak separation. It cannot identify fulfilled needs or guarantee compatibility.

Pseudocode: `counts ← training transitions; residual ← decayed smoothed transition lift; score ← SASRec + λ·residual; optimize sampled-softmax(score)`.

Compare baseline, loss-only, residual-only, and combined variants using macro validation HR@10. Preserve all routing, seeds, splits, evaluator, and diagnostic interfaces; update the predictor writer with identical scoring. Bound experiments by the shared GPU budget. No improvement is claimed.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: At 0% progress, prioritize **recency-weighted transition residual scoring with multi-negative training**, strengthened against target-statistic shortcuts. Benefits remain hypotheses requiring pinned validation.

**Insights and directions.** Three starting-point insights: (1) Electronics’ 14.95% wrong-ranking rate motivates discrimination, but training margins do not establish validation HR@10. (2) Similarity near 0.54 does not establish severe collapse. (3) Users report incidental-history influence and broad relatedness without current purpose, motivating recency and transition evidence.

Three literature insights: (1) [GaGNN](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ins.2024.120623) supports investigating directed associations, without establishing compatibility. (2) [LSIDN](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.14743) motivates separating recent and persistent interests. (3) [eSASRec](https://arxiv.org/html/2508.06450v1) motivates multi-negative objectives, although architecture changes confound attribution. Directions remain transition evidence, temporal relevance, and ranking supervision.

| Framework axis | Existing approach | Proposed role / gap |
|---|---|---|
| Sequence context | SASRec attention | Retain personalization |
| Recent intent | LSIDN encoders | Decay recent sources |
| Directed association | GaGNN graphs | Cross-fitted, smoothed transition lift |
| Discrimination | eSASRec sampled softmax | Explicit candidate-set CE |

**Candidate assessments.** Scores are originality / future potential / user alignment / model alignment / code difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder. Each cell includes a strength and limitation.

| Idea | Originality | Potential | User | Model | Difficulty |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Multi-negative SASRec | 2: direct; established | 7: reusable; incremental | 4: finer ranking; no temporal mechanism | 9: confusion; false negatives | 2: small; masking |
| Gated recent/history fusion | 4: targeted; familiar | 7: extensible; unstable gates | 8: incidental-interest control; prerequisites unresolved | 6: context; indirect separation | 5: modular; inference changes |
| **Recent transition residual + CE** | 4: targeted combination; established parts | 7: reusable; sparse support | 8: recent purpose; compatibility unresolved | 8: discrimination; graph bias | 6: sparse operations; cross-fitting |

**Selected algorithm.** Build training-only adjacent-transition graphs using five user-grouped folds; each training user receives graph statistics excluding their fold. This adapts [CatBoost’s](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.09516) target-statistic safeguards; causal prefixes alone do not prevent graph target exposure. Smooth toward destination popularity, aggregate clipped log-lift over exponentially weighted recent sources, and add it to SASRec scores. Use the full training graph at inference; measure sensitivity to this graph-density shift.

Train with unique uniform unseen negatives and uncorrected candidate-set CE, explicitly distinct from catalog-softmax estimation. Decay targets older/incidental influence; directed evidence targets broad relatedness; CE targets weak separation. Compatibility, fulfilled needs, and latent positives remain unresolved.

Pseudocode: `exclude user fold → build graph; score = SASRec + λ·recent_lift; optimize candidate_CE; infer with full training graph`.

Compare baseline, loss-only, residual-only, and combined arms; retain Markov and recent item-kNN as inexpensive contextual baselines. Predeclare configurations, checkpoints, stopping rules, and budget allocations. Report paired category-stratified bootstrap deltas and interaction, with exploratory intervals: selection optimism remains ([Cawley–Talbot](https://jmlr.csail.mit.edu/papers/v11/cawley10a.html)), and sampled rankings need not transfer to full catalogs ([Krichene–Rendle](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/)). Preserve routing, seeds, splits, evaluator, predictor writer, and diagnostics. No improvement is claimed.
