INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: The strongest early-stage direction is **candidate-conditioned recent-history attention added to SASRec**, tested as a small residual branch. Its benefits remain hypotheses; the supplied inspiration’s 0.5600 score lacks a matched comparison establishing improvement.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec already models temporal dependencies, so another recency mechanism needs an explicit control. (2) User feedback identifies two actionable failures: broad associations crowd out specific matches, and older incidental interactions outweigh recent repeated interests. Candidate-specific evidence selection could address both, without establishing compatibility. (3) Electronics has 14.95% negative-over-positive rankings and a −0.4812 lower-tail margin; modest embedding alignment does not justify prioritizing collapse remedies.

**Insights from related work.** (1) MARS motivates candidate-specific matching of multiple preference signals, but its pretrained text setting exceeds this task’s resources. (2) DARE motivates separate attention and value embeddings; its long-history CTR evidence does not establish transfer here. (3) gSASRec motivates testing sampled-loss calibration before increasing capacity; training-margin improvements still require validation HR@10 confirmation.

The conceptual framework separates **evidence selection** (SASRec attention, candidate attention, interest states), **evidence representation** (ID embeddings or supplied text), and **ranking supervision** (BCE, gBCE, sampled softmax). The selected algorithm changes evidence selection while retaining baseline supervision, making the first experiment easier to interpret.

Scores below are hypotheses. O/P/U/M denote originality, future potential, user-diagnosis alignment, and model-diagnosis alignment; D measures implementation difficulty, with higher meaning harder. Each cell gives one advantage and one limitation.

| Idea | O | P | U | M | D |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Multi-negative gBCE [gSASRec] | 2: focused / established | 7: reusable / sampling-sensitive | 3: discriminates / no intent selection | 8: targets confusion / margins insufficient | 3: encoder unchanged / extra sampling |
| Recent candidate attention [MARS, DARE] | 4: useful adaptation / familiar ingredients | 7: extensible / sparse evidence | 8: specificity and recency / no compatibility labels | 7: candidate discrimination / possible overfit | 5: small branch / scorer synchronization |
| Three causal interest states [D2MFRec] | 4: persistent interests / established routing | 7: richer histories / state instability | 7: recurring interests / completion unobserved | 5: differentiated features / indirect margin link | 7: compact states / causal routing complexity |

Select recent candidate attention at 0% progress. Score each candidate using SASRec plus a bounded residual attending to the last ten valid interactions through independent key/query and value embeddings. Recency weights favor current evidence; candidate conditioning lets different candidates retrieve different supporting events. Neither mechanism guarantees semantic precision.

Compare baseline, recency pooling, and candidate attention at matched residual capacity. Select exclusively by pinned four-category macro validation HR@10. Report existing margin diagnostics and descriptive simulator feedback; claim neither compatibility nor satisfaction gains without evidence.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: The strongest early-stage direction remains **candidate-conditioned recent-history attention added to SASRec**, tested as a small residual branch. Benefits remain hypotheses: the supplied inspiration’s 0.5600 score lacks a matched comparison establishing improvement.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec already selects temporal evidence; another recency mechanism needs a control. (2) Candidate-specific recent evidence targets two observed failures: broad associations crowding out specific matches, and older incidental interactions outweighing recent repeated interests. It cannot establish compatibility or completed needs. (3) Electronics’ 14.95% negative-over-positive rate and −0.4812 lower-tail margin motivate discrimination experiments; modest embedding alignment does not establish collapse.

**Insights from related work.** (1) DIN establishes candidate-conditioned pooling; DARE motivates separate attention/value embeddings, reducing this proposal’s novelty. (2) FPMC/FOSSIL establish asymmetric transition scoring and lag-weighted aggregation; the pooling control is therefore substantive. (3) gSASRec motivates objective calibration as a simpler alternative. MARS motivates richer matching, but its pretrained-text evidence does not transfer directly.

The conceptual framework separates **evidence selection** (sequence attention, lag pooling, candidate attention, interest states), **representation** (shared IDs, independent tables, supplied text), and **supervision** (BCE, gBCE, sampled softmax). The first experiment changes selection and residual representation while retaining baseline supervision.

Scores are hypotheses. O/P/U/M denote originality, future potential, user-diagnosis alignment, and model-diagnosis alignment; D measures difficulty. Each cell gives an advantage and limitation.

| Idea | O | P | U | M | D |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Multi-negative gBCE [gSASRec] | 2: focused / established | 7: reusable / sampling-sensitive | 3: discrimination / no intent selection | 8: confusion target / diagnostic transfer uncertain | 3: encoder retained / sampling cost |
| Recent candidate attention [DIN, DARE, FPMC] | 3: targeted combination / established mechanisms | 6: extensible / sparse evidence | 7: specificity and recency / no compatibility labels | 6: discrimination / bounded influence | 6: short window / scorer and budget complexity |
| Three causal interest states [D2MFRec] | 4: persistence / established routing | 7: richer history / unstable states | 7: recurring interests / completion unobserved | 5: differentiated features / indirect link | 7: compact / causal routing complexity |

Select recent candidate attention at 0% progress for explicit diagnosis alignment and a bounded implementation. Attend over the last ten valid events using independent attention and directional value tables. Compare baseline, exponential pooling, and candidate attention with identical value/target heads; report additional active attention parameters rather than adding unused parameters to claim capacity parity.

Retain fixed α=0.1 initially: because the cosine residual lies in [−1,1], it can change a fixed-backbone pairwise gap by at most 0.2. Record score scale and ranking flips; do not claim this bounds jointly trained model differences. Learned gains remain future work.

Select exclusively by pinned sampled macro validation HR@10. Report every configuration, category results, conditional paired uncertainty when available, and selection optimism. Sampled improvements do not establish full-catalog or satisfaction gains.
