{
  "description": "Test 32-negative softmax training for the unchanged four-dataset SASRec architecture, using baseline BCE-1 and matched-negative BCE-32 controls to distinguish objective effects from increased negative competition.",
  "motivation": "Strong separation against individual sampled training negatives does not establish generalization or identify the cause of broad recommendations. Multiple-negative competition is a feasible early-stage intervention, but gSASRec's matched-negative results do not support assuming softmax superiority. The experiment therefore targets the pinned macro validation HR@10 while treating practical usefulness and full-catalog ranking as separate questions.",
  "implementation_notes": "Retain sasrec_perdataset, Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics and CDs_and_Vinyl; preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], unchanged splits and category-local item mappings, and SASREC_EPOCHS with environment default 200. Preserve baseline architecture, optimizer, regularization, scoring scale, precision and checkpoint rules initially. Plan baseline BCE-1, BCE-32 and softmax-32 with equal declared tuning effort; do not describe BCE-1 versus softmax-32 alone as an isolated loss comparison. Report epochs, updates, GPU seconds and validation queries because matched updates do not imply matched compute. Use a predefined shared-budget allocation and retain time for diagnostics and export verification; incomplete runs must be labeled and included in the cost ledger.\n\nKeep user IDs in internal dataset records or the collator. Never infer identity from truncated sequences. Diagnostic batches retain (None, sequence, positive, negative) with B x L tensors, while training's multiple negatives use a separate B x L x K representation or on-demand position chunks. Define eligible IDs as 1..category_catalog_size excluding the user's complete training-history set and the active target. Do not substitute causal-prefix exclusion, consult validation/test interactions, or sample across categories. Use bounded uniform rejection without replacement, then fill from an exact sorted remaining eligible array using rng.choice(replace=False). Record shortfalls when fewer than 32 items are eligible, mask unused logits, and exclude padding. For zero eligible negatives, use a differentiable zero data loss and report the count. Use dedicated NumPy SeedSequence-derived generators keyed by category, epoch, stable example identifier and position, plus deterministic batch ordering; record software versions. Match draws between BCE-32 and softmax-32, and use the first draw for BCE-1 when this preserves the baseline sampler's uniform exclusion policy.\n\nKeep baseline BCE-1's exact reduction documented. Define BCE-32 per position as [softplus(-positive_logit) + sum(softplus(valid_negative_logits))] / (K_valid + 1), then average over all nonpadding target positions. Separately averaging positive and negative losses would change their relative weights and is a different intervention. Define softmax-32 as cross-entropy over the positive and all valid negatives together, with padding logits masked to negative infinity. Uniform sampled softmax here is a candidate-set objective, not an unbiased full-catalog softmax estimator. Do not split a position's candidate denominator, introduce normalization or temperature tuning, or silently alter the baseline regularizer.\n\nTo bound candidate-scoring memory, compute encoder features H once, create Z = H.detach().requires_grad_(True), zero parameter gradients once, and process valid positions in chunks. For every chunk, freshly look up graph-connected candidate embeddings, compute its summed position losses divided by the total valid-position count N, and backward immediately. This accumulates Z.grad and direct scoring-parameter gradients while freeing the chunk scoring graph. After all chunks, call H.backward(Z.grad), apply the baseline regularizer once where applicable, preserve baseline clipping behavior, and step once. Tied item embeddings must receive both direct candidate and encoder gradients. Never detach candidate embeddings or precompute their graph-connected lookups across chunks. The encoder graph, feature gradients and parameter gradients still occupy memory; this is not constant-memory training. Handle batches with no valid targets explicitly. If mixed precision is used, keep loss scaling consistent across chunk and encoder backward, unscale once, and avoid double scaling the accumulated feature gradient. Verify loss and parameter-gradient equivalence against an unchunked small deterministic batch, including an uneven final chunk and tied embeddings, before spending full training budget.\n\nPredeclare validation cadence, eligible checkpoints, patience, deterministic ties, maximum validation queries and selection across the three complete four-category configurations. Use existing baseline rules where available. Select only by the host's pinned macro validation HR@10 with 200 users per category, 99 unique negatives and evaluation seed 42. Report per-category HR and training-only popularity/history-length slices with sizes; margins and logit spread remain scale-dependent diagnostics. Paired user-bootstrap intervals, when host hit indicators are available, condition on trained models and fixed candidates and do not correct repeated-selection bias. Uniform unobserved negatives may be relevant despite complete-history exclusion; check popularity associations and avoid claims that the intervention resolves compatibility, duplication or intent.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), metrics and metric_definitions, actual trained view.model, item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data and review_data hooks. Preserve bounded embedding and sampled-margin probes, use separate diagnostic random streams and restore every module's prior training state. Return null for unavailable measurements and keep unavailable category lists empty. Produce full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for five real training users per category. Ground qualitative claims in available history IDs, timestamps and exact title/description spans; unavailable reviews, ratings, categories, prices, stores, ownership and simulator traits remain unknown. Explicit feedback is required for immediate-choice claims.\n\nKeep artifacts beneath the trial directory. Record configuration, code, seeds, runtime, negative-exclusion policy and loss reductions; save model and optimizer states, scheduler state where applicable and RNG states for resumption. The inference architecture remains SASRec, but the predictor writer must export the actual selected weights and all four routes. Reload working/predict.py and verify scores within declared precision-appropriate tolerances, top-10 rankings, padding, truncation, item mappings and routing. Charge training, diagnostics, failures and export checks to the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. No validation or full-catalog improvement is established in advance.",
  "pseudocode": "arms = [baseline_BCE_1, BCE_32, softmax_32]\nfor arm in predeclared_budgeted_order:\n    for category in four_fixed_categories:\n        initialize identical baseline SASRec using training seed 42\n        preserve preprocessing seed 0 and baseline epoch environment\n        for epoch in declared_schedule:\n            for internal_batch in deterministic_training_order:\n                users, sequence, positive = internal_batch\n                valid_indices = nonzero(positive != 0)\n                N = len(valid_indices)\n                if N == 0: continue\n                optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\n                H = model.log2feats(sequence)\n                Z = H.detach().requires_grad_(True)\n                for indices in position_chunks(valid_indices):\n                    negatives, mask = sample_uniform_without_replacement(\n                        category_local_catalog,\n                        exclude=complete_training_history_of_each_user_and_target,\n                        K=1 if arm == baseline_BCE_1 else 32,\n                        rng=stable_sample_position_stream(category, epoch, indices))\n                    # Matching 32-negative arms use identical candidates.\n                    # Lookups remain attached and are created inside this chunk.\n                    logits = score_with_fresh_candidate_lookups(Z[indices], positive[indices], negatives)\n                    if arm == softmax_32:\n                        mask_unused_negative_logits_to_minus_infinity(logits, mask)\n                        losses = cross_entropy(logits.float(), positive_class_zero, reduction='none')\n                    elif arm == BCE_32:\n                        losses = (softplus(-logits[:, 0]) + sum_masked(softplus(logits[:, 1:]), mask)) / (1 + mask.sum(-1))\n                    else:\n                        losses = exact_baseline_BCE_reduction_per_position(logits, mask)\n                    losses = zero_data_loss_for_positions_without_eligible_negatives(losses, mask)\n                    (losses.sum() / N).backward()\n                H.backward(Z.grad)\n                backward_baseline_regularizer_once_if_present()\n                apply_baseline_gradient_clipping_if_present()\n                optimizer.step()\n            save_and_validate_only_at_declared_checkpoints()\n    evaluate_complete_four_category_configuration_with_pinned_macro_HR10()\nselect_configuration_using_declared_macro_validation_and_tie_rules()\nrun_bounded_compatible_diagnostics_and_required_training_user_recommendations()\nexport_selected_four_models_through_predictor_writer()\nreload_export_and_verify_scores_top10_masks_item_ids_and_dataset_routing()\nrecord_all_costs_in_shared_search_budget()",
