INFO:self_evolverec:The new idea for model in iteration 3:
{
  "description": "Add a bounded candidate-conditioned directional residual to each dataset’s SASRec. Select evidence from the ten most recent valid prefix events using an independent attention embedding table, then score selected evidence with separate source-value and candidate-target tables. Preserve the baseline encoder, objective, optimizer, and negative sampling in the initial comparison. Strengthen the previous specification by scaling cosine attention logits, defining reproducible controls and scoring interfaces, and accounting for full-catalog cost.",
  "motivation": "The idea explicitly targets two user-facing failures: broad associations without specific current evidence, and distant incidental interactions outweighing recent repeated interests. Candidate-specific selection and a restricted causal window provide corresponding inductive biases, although either can fail. Separate directional values also target Electronics’ weak sampled training discrimination: 10.88% ranking reversals and a −0.0915 tenth-percentile margin. Mildly positive base-embedding cosine alignment is not evidence of collapse and does not motivate a collapse penalty. Compatibility, fulfilled needs, redundant alternatives, and satisfaction remain unresolved because their labels are unavailable. Originality remains 3/10; future potential is revised from 7 to 6 because the approach overlaps substantially with DARE and incurs candidate-dependent inference work. Code difficulty rises from 5 to 6 because diagnosis compatibility, scoring shapes, and full-catalog execution require substantial care.",
  "implementation_notes": "Prespecify K=10, branch dimension d=32, normalization epsilon 1e-6, fixed cosine multiplier g=sqrt(32), recency factor 0.8, and fixed residual coefficient alpha=1.0. These are engineering hypotheses, not established optima. Relative to the previous report, multiplying only cosine by g deliberately changes its strength relative to recency. With g=1, the nine-lag penalty is approximately 2.008, exceeding cosine’s full range of 2; an oldest maximally similar event cannot outrank a newest maximally dissimilar event. Scaling the entire logit would preserve that ordering. QKNorm motivates considering a multiplier but does not establish sqrt(32) as optimal here. Do not add a temperature or recency sweep to this initial comparison.\n\nUse independent trainable attention A, source S, and target T embedding tables with padding index zero. Initialize nonpadding rows independently from Normal(0, 1/d), where 1/d is the variance, yielding expected squared row norm one; explicitly zero padding rows. Normalize retrieved vectors as x/max(||x||_2, epsilon). This avoids deliberately tiny initial norms, although normalization gradients still depend on raw norms. Compute attention logits g*cos(A_i,A_j)+lag*log(0.8), and directional values cos(S_i,T_j). Their attention-weighted average lies in [-1,1], bounding the added score by alpha. Mask padding after transformations. Implement all-masked rows by returning zero directly, avoiding softmax over entirely negative-infinite logits. Candidate item zero receives no residual. Repeated events retain distinct positions; this is not a learned measure of semantic recurrence.\n\nAt training position t, gather at most ten valid events at positions at or before t, including sequence[t]. Assign lag zero to the newest valid event and increasing integer lags to older valid events. In normally left-padded contiguous sequences this matches positional lag; explicitly handle internal padding if present. Neither the next-item target nor a future suffix enters this window. Compute candidate attention separately for positive and negative targets, and exclude padded positive positions from loss. An observed repeated interaction may be a legitimate target; do not newly mask such targets or change baseline negative sampling for this experiment.\n\nConstruct and initialize the branch after the baseline inside an RNG-preserving context, including applicable CUDA RNG state. The unchanged baseline configuration bypasses all branch construction and optimizer registration. Keep the baseline initialization order, regularization expressions, sampler, batch size, optimizer settings, and stochastic encoder operations. Include new tables in the same optimizer with its existing defaults; apply any baseline table-specific regularization only to its original parameters and add no new explicit branch penalty. Record these settings in saved configuration rather than assuming seeds alone preserve equivalence. The branch has no stochastic forward operations.\n\nPrespecify exactly three configurations: unchanged SASRec; a directional residual with weights proportional to 0.8^lag and no attention table; and the same residual with candidate-conditioned attention. Both residual configurations use identical S/T initialization, dimensions, windows, coefficient, normalization, encoder settings, training objective, and negative sampler. Attention adds (number_of_items+1)*32 parameters, so this comparison measures the combined effect of selection and extra capacity. It does not isolate selection at matched capacity. Wider SASRec, wider pooling, independent learned pooling, tied versus separate S/T, tied versus independent A, uniform pooling, and last-item pooling are future controls requiring retraining and equal tuning allowances. Do not silently add them to the current search.\n\nObtain actual remaining GPU budget from existing host accounting at execution time; no remaining amount is supplied here. All previous and new training, validation, diagnostics, debugging, and failures count toward the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Before additional runs, reserve 20% of the actual remaining budget for diagnostics and failures and divide the remainder equally among the three configurations, including any required baseline rerun. Use charged baseline timing to freeze a common epoch cap that fits each allocation, never exceeding int(os.environ.get('SASREC_EPOCHS', '200')). Reuse a previous baseline only when architecture, initialization, data processing, training schedule, and completed epoch cap match exactly. If allocations cannot support a common comparison across all four categories, report that limitation instead of claiming a completed experiment. Select only final common-epoch checkpoints; avoid extra checkpoint or hyperparameter selection. The largest unrounded host macro validation HR@10 wins, with baseline preferred on ties and recency-only pooling preferred on remaining ties.\n\nRetain Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics, and CDs_and_Vinyl with original routing, splits, preprocessing seed 0, and training seed 42. DATA_DIR remains os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']; save artifacts beneath the trial directory. Execute training on one GPU. Do not access hidden test files, alter the host evaluator, fetch pretrained weights, or add datasets. Contrary to the generic metadata examples in the input, reviews, ratings, product categories, prices, stores, and simulator traits are unavailable. Titles, descriptions, and timestamps are permitted but unnecessary for this first experiment.\n\nCandidate-conditioned attention generally cannot be reduced to a single candidate-independent dot product. Make log2feats(sequence) return a documented feature bundle containing encoder features, recent item IDs, validity masks, and lags. Adapt all existing consumers, including diagnostics, rather than assuming the former tensor interface remains valid. Preserve predict_score(features, items) with a documented default: rank-3 encoder features use aligned B×L target scoring, and rank-2 final-prefix features use B×C candidate-list scoring. If additional modes are needed, expose an explicit optional mode argument; never infer semantics solely from B×L versus B×C item shapes, since L and C may coincide. For candidate lists, select the final valid prefix explicitly; an empty sequence has a zero branch output and follows the baseline’s documented base-score handling.\n\nCache recent normalized A/S vectors once per prefix and score candidates in fixed chunks of 256, retaining only running top recommendations when full-catalog output does not require all scores. Use all unseen catalog items for the twenty simulator users, five per category; chunking must not become approximate retrieval or candidate pruning. Mask seen training-history items according to the native simulator contract. Complexity is O(MKd) for the branch, versus O(MH) for baseline catalog scoring after encoding. Chunking limits memory but does not reduce total work. Record model parameter counts, charged GPU time, peak GPU memory, and end-to-end full-catalog recommendation latency for the required twenty users. Final held-out inference remains separately recorded by the host as specified.\n\nPreserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions, with view.model referencing the actual trained nn.Module and view.item_emb the baseline item embedding. Compute margins through the complete scorer under eval/no_grad, restoring the original training mode afterward. Keep base-table cosine measurements explicitly labeled as directional alignment rather than whole-model collapse. Preserve empty category breakdowns because item categories are unavailable. On the same bounded diagnostic batches, record attention entropy, effective attended-history count, raw table norm summaries, residual-score distribution, and residual-to-base score scale; label unavailable attention metrics for baseline or recency-only configurations. Monitor branch gradient norms during already scheduled training steps rather than running extra optimization. None of these diagnostics replaces validation HR@10.\n\nUpdate the predictor writer to contain the identical architecture and serialize architecture version, baseline configuration, all branch state, initialization-independent inference settings, K, d, epsilon, g, recency factor, alpha, feature-bundle contract, and dataset routing. Verify score agreement before and after reload, aligned-versus-single-candidate scoring, candidate-chunk equivalence, empty-history behavior, padding invariance, score bounds, and prefix causality on public histories. Identical-history source/target asymmetry is learned rather than guaranteed; symmetric attention pair scores plus causal windows do not establish a complementary relation.\n\nTo detect reliance on attention, optionally replace candidate attention queries with a deterministic permutation of candidates on the existing diagnostic batch while preserving each candidate’s target vector; alternatively use recency-only weights in the fitted attention model. Report score changes and margins descriptively. These inference interventions do not substitute for retrained controls and cannot prove semantic evidence selection. The branch can learn popularity, common transitions, frequent-item hubs, or spurious associations. Restricting the window can erase older recurring preferences, and softmax always allocates unit mass over nonempty histories even when no event supports a candidate. Bounded values limit magnitude but do not provide confidence-based abstention. Lexical grounding, suppression states, and confidence gates remain future hypotheses.\n\nPreserve the pinned validation protocol: 200 users/category, 99 unique negatives, evaluation seed 42, and equal-weight macro HR@10. One changed hit moves category HR by 0.5 percentage points and macro HR by 0.125 points. If the unchanged host exposes per-user hits on identical candidate sets, jointly resample user indices across models within each category for 10,000 replicates using a separate NumPy generator seeded 42; recompute macro differences and report percentile 95% intervals, category differences, and discordant-hit counts. Otherwise state that paired uncertainty estimates are unavailable; do not modify or reproduce the evaluator to obtain them. Intervals condition on fitted models, observed users, and fixed candidates, exclude training-seed and candidate variability, and do not correct adaptive-search optimism. Under independent Bernoulli sampling, worst-case standard errors are approximately 3.54 percentage points/category and 1.77 points/macro; this approximation is not an uncertainty estimate for paired model differences. Sampled HR rankings can differ from full-catalog rankings. Simulator feedback from twenty users remains descriptive and must not trigger further tuning in this prespecified comparison.",
  "pseudocode": "CONFIGS = [baseline, recency_only, candidate_attention]\nK, d, eps = 10, 32, 1e-6\ng, decay, alpha = sqrt(32), 0.8, 1.0\nfreeze_budget_and_common_epoch_cap_from_actual_host_records()\n\nfor config in CONFIGS:\n    for category in ORIGINAL_FOUR_CATEGORIES:\n        set_training_seed(42)\n        model = construct_original_sasrec()\n        if config != baseline:\n            with preserve_rng_state():\n                initialize S, T independently with nonpadding N(0, variance=1/d)\n                if config == candidate_attention:\n                    initialize independent A identically\n                zero all branch padding rows\n\n        function log2feats(sequence):\n            h = baseline_encoder(sequence)\n            windows, mask, lag = last_K_valid_causal_events(sequence, K)\n            return FeatureBundle(h, windows, mask, lag)\n\n        function predict_score(features, items, mode=None):\n            # Default: rank-3 h -> aligned B,L; rank-2 h -> candidates B,C.\n            mode = documented_mode_from_feature_rank_if_unspecified(mode)\n            base = original_baseline_score(features.h, items, mode)\n            if config == baseline:\n                return base\n            values = shape_checked_dot(normalize(S(features.windows), eps),\n                                       normalize(T(items), eps), mode)\n            if config == candidate_attention:\n                logits = g * shape_checked_dot(normalize(A(features.windows), eps),\n                                               normalize(A(items), eps), mode)\n            else:\n                logits = shape_compatible_zeros(values)\n            logits += log(decay) * features.lag\n            weights = masked_softmax_returning_zero_for_empty_rows(logits,\n                                                                  features.mask)\n            residual = sum_over_history(weights * values)\n            residual *= items.ne(0)\n            return base + alpha * residual\n\n        for epoch in range(COMMON_EPOCH_CAP):\n            for _, sequence, positive, negative in original_train_loader:\n                f = log2feats(sequence)\n                pos = predict_score(f, positive, mode='aligned')\n                neg = predict_score(f, negative, mode='aligned')\n                optimize_original_loss_and_regularization(pos, neg,\n                                                         valid=positive.ne(0))\n        save_complete_config_state_and_routing_under_trial_directory()\n\n    write_working_predictor_with_identical_architecture_and_chunk_size_256()\n    verify_reload_shapes_padding_bounds_causality_and_chunk_equivalence()\n    run_compatible_complete_scorer_diagnostics_within_reserved_budget()\n    obtain_validation_scores_from_unchanged_host()\n\nselect_by_unrounded_macro_HR10_with_prespecified_ties()\n# No additional tuning from training margins or simulator feedback.\n# Report paired uncertainty only if unchanged host exposes paired user hits.",
