{
  "description": "Compare unchanged four-dataset SASRec trained with BCE-1, balanced BCE-32 and restricted-candidate softmax-32 using paired initialization, examples and negative pools, with bounded controls for optimization scale and popularity shortcuts.",
  "motivation": "Existing diagnostics show separation from sampled training negatives, particularly strong in Movies_and_TV and weaker in Electronics, but do not establish validation ranking quality. BCE-32 versus BCE-1 tests the effect of averaging additional negatives; softmax-32 versus BCE-32 compares objectives under matched exposure. Qualitative failures motivate the experiment without establishing that loss choice causes unsupported associations or redundant recommendations. At 10% research progress, this controlled, architecture-preserving experiment remains preferable to adding an untested architectural mechanism.",
  "implementation_notes": "Preserve DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, evaluation seed 42, all four categories, existing item mappings, dataset routing and unchanged splits. Respect SASREC_EPOCHS and its host default of 200. Write checkpoints, configurations, diagnostics and exports beneath the trial directory. Use one GPU per trial; all training, failures and diagnostics count toward the aggregate 43,200 GPU-second budget.\n\nSampler: define each user's eligible set as nonpadding catalog IDs excluding that user's entire training-item set, including training events after the current prefix. This matches the supplied original SASRec sampler policy; it is not a prefix-only exclusion policy. Never consult validation or test interactions for eligibility. Draw an ordered uniform sample without replacement of K=min(32, eligible_count) items independently per target position. Repeated items across positions are permitted. BCE-1 receives the first member, whose marginal distribution is uniform over eligible IDs. Record eligible counts, actual K, zero-eligible positions and skipped empty batches. Avoid rejection loops when eligibility is empty or small. Use deterministic complement sampling or bounded rejection with an exact uniform fallback; cache complements only when memory permits. Existing one-negative loader tensors can remain unchanged; generate the auxiliary pool through a separate sampler using the batch's training-user identity or an aligned side channel. Do not reconstruct user identity from potentially nonunique sequence tensors.\n\nLosses: retain positions with nonzero sequence and positive IDs; a missing negative does not invalidate a positive target. BCE-1 is mean[softplus(-positive)+softplus(first_negative)], with zero negative contribution when K=0. BCE-32 replaces the negative term with its within-position mean over K valid negatives. This preserves the original SASRec data-loss scaling at K=1; averaging all positive and negative terms together would change it. Compute BCE only from finite logits and mask finite unreduced negative losses before summation. Restricted softmax uses the positive plus valid negatives, temperature 1, invalid negative logits set to negative infinity, and logsumexp-minus-positive. Remove padding rows first. K=0 gives exactly zero softmax loss and gradient, retained in the valid-target average; BCE remains positive-only. Skip batches containing no valid targets and report this boundary-case difference. Apply identical, explicitly documented baseline regularization, reductions, dropout, optimizer settings and clipping rules. Document whether regularization is a loss penalty or optimizer weight decay, including parameter groups and Adam versus AdamW semantics. No logQ correction is used, and the objective is not claimed to be an unbiased full-catalog likelihood.\n\nPairing: initialize each arm from the same saved model state and identically initialized optimizer state for each dataset. Reproduce minibatch order, negative pools and model-dropout RNG streams by resetting or restoring independent states at each arm's start. Use separate generators for auxiliary sampling and diagnostics so changing negative count does not change dropout or data order. Log initial-state hashes and verify identical evaluation-mode scores before updates. Generate pools deterministically from dataset, epoch and batch identity, or persist their sampler state. Validate uniqueness, exclusion, ID bounds and masks. Score valid training positions in chunks when necessary; normalize chunk sums by the total valid-target count, apply regularization once per optimizer update and keep clipping at the original update boundary.\n\nControls and interpretation: BCE-32 versus BCE-1 changes gradient variance and negative coverage, not only 'competition'; their expected negative BCE term is the same under uniform sampling. Softmax-32 versus BCE-32 changes candidate coupling, absolute-score anchoring and gradient allocation. At all-zero logits with K=32, CE gradients are 64/33 times balanced BCE gradients. Include a constant CE multiplier of 33/64 as a bounded initialization-scale control; no constant equates these losses away from equal logits. Adam can cancel much constant scaling, while epsilon, clipping and regularization complicate equivalence. Temperature changes relative hardness as well as scale, so learning-rate adjustment cannot reproduce it. The primary comparison keeps temperature 1. Report a fixed-configuration result as such; if budget remains, prioritize the same small learning-rate grid for BCE-32 and CE-32 before one predeclared CE temperature check. Match update opportunities for scientific comparisons, report actual GPU time separately, and use equal allocated compute for any tuning comparison. Record all incomplete and failed arms rather than hiding budget-driven truncation.\n\nDiagnostics: preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), metrics and metric_definitions, and view.model as the actual trained nn.Module. Retain item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data and review_data compatibility fields. Existing one-negative margin and embedding-alignment probes remain bounded auxiliary measurements, with the same fixed diagnostic examples and negatives across arms. Architecture-specific residual probes remain inapplicable. Add bounded fixed-pool measurements of top-1/top-5 shares of absolute negative-logit gradients, encoder/item-embedding gradient norms and per-parameter RMS, clipping frequency, score distributions and embedding norms. For K=0 or a zero total negative derivative, concentration is null rather than zero; report K alongside concentration. Use analytic derivatives or an isolated no-update probe, exclude regularization from logit-gradient concentration, and restore RNG states and module modes afterward. Tied embedding gradients mix sequence-input and candidate-scoring paths; label aggregate measurements accordingly unless separate path attribution is implemented. These probes do not establish intent, semantic compatibility or representation collapse.\n\nSelection and uncertainty: retain the pinned 200 validation users/category and 99 unique negative candidates, without changing the host evaluator. Before launching, declare checkpoint cadence, eligibility, patience and deterministic ties using baseline rules where available; write the effective rules and allocated GPU time into the trial configuration. Select only by the four-category macro validation HR@10, retaining one jointly selected four-route predictor rather than silently choosing each category's best epoch. If host outputs expose per-user hits, record paired binary outcomes on identical candidates; otherwise mark paired analyses unavailable rather than substituting another evaluator. Use 10,000 bootstrap replicates with diagnostic seed 1042, resampling 200 users independently within each category while preserving model pairing; equally average four category differences per replicate and report percentile 95% intervals, category intervals and win/loss/tie counts. These intervals condition on fitted models, candidates and observed users; they omit training-seed, split and candidate-draw uncertainty and do not correct adaptive selection. Record every trial, validation query, checkpoint and configuration, labeling comparisons exploratory until the host's untouched final evaluation.\n\nShortcut analysis and qualitative evidence: define popularity from training interaction counts, including zero-count catalog items. Freeze category-specific item-count tertile boundaries before validation analysis and report cutpoints, ties and stratum sizes. Report paired validation changes and target counts by stratum, plus a training-popularity-only baseline on pinned candidates when supported by the host. Do not select checkpoints from slices. Retain full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for five real training users/category; record mean log(1+training_count), head share, coverage and item exposure concentration using a fixed list length and deterministic ties. Treat these twenty users as descriptive. Audit candidate/history IDs, timestamps and exact available title/description spans; label compatibility, complementarity and redundancy claims supported, contradicted or unknown. Missing requirements or current intent remain unknown. Preserve simulator-feedback provenance and never fabricate reviews, ratings, categories, prices, stores or diversity/conformity traits. Sampled HR gains and popularity diagnostics cannot establish full-catalog accuracy or causal preference improvement.\n\nExport and reproducibility: keep the unchanged architecture and exact scoring configuration in the predictor writer. Save selected weights, objective/sampler configuration, item mappings and routing; save optimizer, scheduler and RNG states when supporting resumption. Record software versions, precision/backend settings, effective hyperparameters and actual updates/GPU time. Reload the generated working/predict.py and verify per-category scores within predeclared precision-appropriate tolerances, top-10 rankings, padding, truncation, IDs and routing. Investigate ranking differences. Reserve budget for export checks and stop when the aggregate budget is exhausted even if planned comparisons remain incomplete.",
  "pseudocode": "import torch\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\ndef objective(pos_logits, neg_logits, neg_mask, mode):\n    # Padding targets have already been removed.\n    # pos_logits: [N]; neg_logits and bool neg_mask: [N, M].\n    # All supplied logits must be finite, including unused placeholders.\n    # Return None for an empty target batch: caller skips optimizer update.\n    if pos_logits.numel() == 0:\n        return None\n    if not torch.isfinite(pos_logits).all() or not torch.isfinite(neg_logits).all():\n        raise ValueError('Nonfinite input logits')\n    counts = neg_mask.sum(dim=-1)\n    positive_loss = F.softplus(-pos_logits)\n    if mode == 'bce1':\n        # Ordered masks place valid samples first; first member is uniform.\n        negative_loss = torch.zeros_like(pos_logits)\n        if neg_logits.shape[1] > 0:\n            negative_loss = torch.where(\n                counts > 0, F.softplus(neg_logits[:, 0]), negative_loss)\n        per_target = positive_loss + negative_loss\n    elif mode == 'bce32':\n        finite_losses = F.softplus(neg_logits)\n        negative_sum = finite_losses.masked_fill(~neg_mask, 0.0).sum(-1)\n        per_target = positive_loss + negative_sum / counts.clamp_min(1)\n    elif mode in ('softmax32', 'softmax32_scaled'):\n        masked_negative_logits = neg_logits.masked_fill(~neg_mask, -torch.inf)\n        candidates = torch.cat([pos_logits[:, None], masked_negative_logits], -1)\n        per_target = torch.logsumexp(candidates, dim=-1) - pos_logits\n        # Make the specified K=0 value and gradient explicit.\n        per_target = torch.where(counts > 0, per_target, torch.zeros_like(per_target))\n        if mode == 'softmax32_scaled':\n            per_target = per_target * (33.0 / 64.0)\n    else:\n        raise ValueError(mode)\n    return per_target.mean()\n\n# For each of the four datasets:\n# 1. Preserve baseline preprocessing/mappings and save one initial model state.\n# 2. Build eligibility from each user's complete TRAINING history only.\n# 3. Generate the same ordered unique pools for every arm using a separate RNG.\n#    K=min(32, eligible_count); handle K=0 without rejection loops.\n# 4. Restore paired initialization, optimizer initialization and training RNG state.\n# 5. Remove padding targets, score positives/pools, and compute selected objective.\n#    Add unchanged baseline regularization once; backward, clip and update normally.\n# 6. Keep matched update/checkpoint opportunities; record actual GPU costs.\n# 7. Compare BCE32-BCE1 for negative averaging/exposure and CE32-BCE32 for\n#    complete objective effects. A constant-rescaled CE arm probes initial scale.\n# 8. Run bounded diagnostics without updating weights or consuming training RNG.\n# 9. Select a four-category checkpoint bundle only by pinned macro HR@10.\n# 10. If available, bootstrap paired per-user hit differences within categories.\n# 11. Export through the predictor writer; reload and verify scores/rankings/routes.\n# Stop at the shared 43,200 GPU-second limit; report unfinished comparisons.",
