INFO:self_evolverec:Research reports (2 report(s)):
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: The best early-stage direction is a **support-gated transition residual with normalized sampled softmax**. It preserves SASRec while testing whether independently supported next-item associations improve current-purpose matching.

**Insights from the starting point.**

1. Directed transitions distinguish next-step evidence from broad similarity. They remain behavioral proxies, not proof of compatibility.
2. Cross-fitting removes a user's contribution to their training graph. It prevents direct self-support but cannot eliminate unobserved-positive ambiguity.
3. Recency can prioritize current interests. However, existing recent-removal sensitivity already exceeds distant-removal sensitivity, so stronger recency is not automatically justified.

**Insights from related work.**

1. Sampled-softmax analysis links normalization and temperature to discriminative gradients [1]. Electronics' 7.9% negative-win rate motivates testing this objective without claiming causality.
2. Directed graph projections provide interaction-only association estimates [2]. Support shrinkage can limit unreliable sparse edges.
3. FAME separates preference components through gated predictions [3]. This offers an alternative when a single sequence representation mixes interests.

**Framework.** Three directions separate the intervention: objective calibration changes discrimination; directed evidence changes association specificity; intent routing changes history aggregation. Cross-fitting and validation selection apply across them. None establishes functional compatibility from interaction order alone.

Scores below are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Each assessment gives a positive and a limitation.

| Atomic idea | Scores | Assessment by dimension |
|---|---|---|
| A: Normalized sampled softmax [1] | 3/7/5/9/2 | Originality: useful adaptation / established loss. Potential: reusable / transfer uncertain. User: sharper ranking / no explicit current-purpose evidence. Model: targets separation / training margins may mislead. Difficulty: small change / sampler care required. |
| B: Support-gated transitions plus A [1,2] | 5/8/8/8/5 | Originality: combines independent evidence and calibration / familiar components. Potential: interpretable residual / sparse coverage. User: recent next-step support / cannot verify compatibility. Model: stronger candidate competition / graph may mask encoder weakness. Difficulty: sparse operations / fold bookkeeping. |
| C: Two gated intent heads [3] | 4/7/7/6/6 | Originality: compact adaptation / established routing. Potential: separates interests / limited sparse-history evidence. User: reduces interest mixing / no complementarity guarantee. Model: richer representation / no direct margin correction. Difficulty: few heads / routing instability. |

**Selected algorithm: B.** At 10% progress, its modest scope and explicit evidence make it preferable. It addresses broadly plausible but nonspecific recommendations and older-interest carryover by adding bounded evidence from recent directed associations. Sampled softmax addresses weak Electronics separation.

Build user-disjoint training graphs; shrink transition lift by source and edge support. Aggregate scores over the latest five prefix items with normalized recency weights. Add a bounded, initially near-zero residual to normalized SASRec logits; unsupported candidates retain backbone scores. Preserve the unrestricted history encoder.

Compare baseline, A, and B under matched budgets. Select exclusively by pinned four-category macro validation HR@10. Extend diagnostics to measure complete scores and graph-removal effects; report no anticipated gain as measured.

References: [1](https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf), [2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09209), [3](https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.01457).
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: The best early-stage direction remains a **support-gated transition residual with candidate-set cross-entropy**, with normalization tested separately. Correct user-level exclusion and objective controls take priority over expanding the encoder.

**Insights from the starting point.**

1. Directed transitions provide next-step evidence beyond broad similarity, but cannot establish compatibility or unsatisfied needs.
2. Cross-fitting prevents training targets from supporting their own graph features. Known users must retain their exclusion graph at inference; averaging all fold graphs restores self-support.
3. Recency may emphasize current interests, but recent-removal sensitivity already exceeds distant-removal sensitivity. Additional recency weighting needs validation.

**Insights from related work.**

1. Sampled-softmax analysis motivates discrimination and temperature controls [1]. Electronics’ 7.9% negative-win rate supports testing objectives, without establishing causality.
2. Sequential Rules and PCTM already combine directed transitions, recency, and popularity adjustments [2,3]. Novelty is therefore modest; sparse-edge reliability remains a useful question.
3. FAME motivates separating preferences [4], while gSASRec provides an objective alternative [5]. Neither establishes transfer to this benchmark.

**Framework.** Objective calibration changes discrimination; directed evidence changes association specificity; intent routing changes history aggregation. User-level exclusion and pinned validation govern all three. Behavioral associations cannot verify functional compatibility.

Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.

| Atomic idea | Scores | Assessment by dimension |
|---|---|---|
| A: Candidate CE; normalization ablation [1,5] | 3/7/5/9/3 | Originality: useful adaptation / established objective. Potential: reusable / transfer uncertain. User: sharper competition / no purpose evidence. Model: targets separation / training margins can mislead. Difficulty: compact / sampler controls matter. |
| B: Support-gated transitions plus A [2,3] | 4/7/8/8/6 | Originality: reliability and exclusion combination / estimator largely anticipated. Potential: interpretable evidence / sparse coverage. User: recent next-step support / compatibility unverified. Model: candidate competition / residual may hide encoder weakness. Difficulty: sparse implementation / exclusion and diagnostics require integration. |
| C: Two gated intent heads [4] | 4/7/7/6/6 | Originality: compact adaptation / established routing. Potential: separates interests / sparse-history uncertainty. User: reduces mixing / no complementarity guarantee. Model: richer features / no direct calibration. Difficulty: few heads / unstable routing. |

**Selected algorithm: B.** At 10% progress, retain this feasible hypothesis while testing A first. It addresses nonspecific recommendations and older-interest carryover through bounded recent transition evidence; CE targets weak Electronics separation. These are intended mechanisms, not demonstrated improvements.

Partition users into fixed folds. Build each graph entirely outside its fold, including normalization statistics; use that same graph for its users throughout training and inference. Apply smoothed positive log-lift with separate edge/source shrinkage, aggregate five recent prefix items, and add an initially small bounded residual. Preserve unrestricted SASRec history.

Compare SASRec-only, objective-only, graph-only, and combined scores within matched budgets. Separate normalization, smoothing, and shrinkage ablations. Select exclusively by pinned four-category macro validation HR@10; report category results, graph coverage, complete-score margins, graph-removal effects, memory, and latency. No gain is yet measured.

References: [1](https://wujcan.github.io/papers/tois-ssm.pdf), [2](https://github.com/rn5l/session-rec/blob/master/algorithms/baselines/sr.py), [3](https://github.com/spotify-research/sequential-capacity-probes), [4](https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.01457), [5](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.07192).
