  "description": "Add a bounded, support-shrunk directed-transition residual to per-dataset SASRec, using the same user-excluded graph during training and inference. Test multi-negative candidate-set cross-entropy first; evaluate embedding normalization and temperature separately rather than attributing their combined effects to the objective.",
  "motivation": "The user diagnosis identifies broadly plausible recommendations lacking a specific next-step connection and continued influence from older interests. Recent transitions supported by other users provide a testable behavioral signal for both failures. They do not establish compatibility, missing capabilities, satisfied needs, or actual purchase intent. Electronics has a training negative-win rate of 0.079 and a 10th-percentile margin of 0.2522, motivating objective controls. Recent-history removal already changes encoder logits substantially more than distant-history removal; stronger recency and embedding isotropy are hypotheses, not established remedies. Sequential Rules and PCTM substantially anticipate the transition estimator, reducing the originality assessment. At 10% research progress, a staged objective-first comparison remains preferable to adding candidate attention or multiple intent experts immediately.",
  "implementation_notes": "Retain four independent dataset models and routing, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts, and the SASREC_EPOCHS environment default of 200. Keep execution single-GPU and account for all training, diagnostics, debugging, and failed trials within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Do not change splits, evaluator candidates, or evaluation seeds; never read hidden test data or use held-out labels for graph construction or negative filtering.\n\nCross-fitting: use K=5 deterministic user folds, assigned from a stable digest of dataset name, user identifier, and seed 0; do not use Python's randomized hash. Persist assignments. For each fold, construct a graph using only other folds' training histories, excluding the held-out group's contributions from every count, prior, pruning decision, and auxiliary statistic. A known user uses this same exclusion graph for every training prefix and at inference. Do not average all five graphs: four contain that user's training transitions. This historical self-support is distinct from held-out-label leakage, but breaks the intended exclusion and changes features. A genuinely unseen user receives a deterministic fold assignment using the same rule; do not introduce a full-data fallback. Repeated-partition ensembles are deferred; if later tested, each branch must exclude the user's group and have matching training construction.\n\nGraph definition: use adjacent ordered training events only, distance weight 1, preserving repeated events and self-transitions. For the relevant exclusion graph, c_ij is the number of i-to-j events, c_i=sum_j c_ij, d_j=sum_i c_ij, and N=sum_i c_i. Let I be the dataset item vocabulary size, excluding padding, and p_j=(d_j+eta)/(N+eta*I). Start with eta=1, alpha=10, beta=5, gamma=20. For observed edges, W_ij=min(5, max(0, log(((c_ij+alpha*p_j)/(c_i+alpha))/p_j))) * c_ij/(c_ij+beta) * c_i/(c_i+gamma). Unobserved edges have weight zero. These are proposed defaults, not values attributed to prior work or established optima. The floor makes the log-lift PMI-like; exact PPMI equivalence requires consistent unsmoothed transition-event marginals. Source and destination counts must come from the same exclusion graph. When N=0, return an all-zero residual.\n\nFor every causal training position, aggregate its last five nonpadding input items, including that position, with weights proportional to 2^(-r/2), where r=0 is the newest retained item. Normalize over available items. Repeated source items contribute at each occurrence. The aggregated graph score lies in [0,5]. Add lambda times this score to backbone logits, with lambda=2*sigmoid(a), initialized to lambda=0.01. Keep the full-history SASRec branch. A scalar lambda is trained independently for each dataset; do not add a complex gate initially. Validate the residual scale and use a fixed prespecified ablation lambda=0. Structural lack of graph support means a zero residual, not evidence that a candidate is unsuitable.\n\nObjective and sampler: start with 64 uniform unique negatives without replacement from nonpadding item IDs excluding the target and all items in the user's training history. Sample min(64, eligible_count), mask unused candidate slots, and skip positions with no eligible negatives while reporting their count. Preserve user identifiers internally in the training sampler or a side channel; the diagnostic loader must continue yielding (None, sequence, positive, negative). Never recover users by matching potentially identical sequences. Use the same sampled candidate batches across objective controls when feasible, with identical initialization, optimizer, filtering, batch schedule, and tuning budget. Mean candidate CE over valid positions is discrimination within sampled candidate sets, not an unbiased full-catalog softmax estimator. Sampling without replacement is specified explicitly; do not silently deduplicate with-replacement samples or apply a with-replacement importance formula. Padding and target collisions are forbidden. History filtering changes the training task relative to evaluation and is a limitation to measure, not a reason to modify the evaluator.\n\nStage controls to fit the budget: reproduce the existing baseline, then compare 64-negative BCE and candidate CE under unchanged dot-product logits and shared sampling. Reduce BCE by averaging negative terms per position before averaging valid positions, and document the positive/negative weighting. Candidate CE has its shared denominator, so numerical loss magnitudes are not directly comparable. If the objective warrants further study, test cosine normalization and temperature separately, starting at tau=0.2, then add the graph to the selected objective configuration. A gBCE control should follow its published positive calibration exactly; gBCE is not importance-corrected softmax. Corrected softmax and hard-negative mining are deferred rather than bundled into the initial experiment. Temperature changes training gradients despite preserving rankings for a fixed normalized model. Preserve the baseline optimizer and its hyperparameters until a separately budgeted optimizer ablation is justified.\n\nValidation and overfitting: use only the host's pinned four-category macro validation HR@10 for selection, preserving 200 validation users/category, 99 unique negatives, and evaluation seed 42. Prespecify comparisons and keep a trial ledger to limit adaptive validation overfitting. Do not claim full-catalog HR or infer causal improvements from training margins. Report category HR and paired user-level uncertainty only if permitted evaluator outputs expose per-user hit indicators; do not reconstruct unavailable results. Bootstrap uncertainty conditional on these fixed candidate sets does not account for repeated model selection. Full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for the twenty simulator users remain a separate qualitative assessment.\n\nSparse implementation: initially store every observed positive-weight edge in sorted sparse rows; missing edges return zero. Use binary search or hashing for batched candidate lookup. If memory requires top-K row pruning, declare K before comparison, record target-edge coverage before and after pruning, and persist the pruned graph used in both training and inference. For full-catalog recommendations, scatter-add the five weighted graph rows into catalog buffers and combine with chunked neural scoring; use the host's existing seen-item exclusion behavior. Record per-category edge count, memory, scoring latency, held-out validation target-edge coverage only as a diagnostic, zero-residual frequency, and training support/degree strata. Do not use validation targets to modify graphs or choose pruning edges.\n\nSerialization: update the predictor writer to save and restore model architecture, item-ID mapping, dataset routing, score normalization mode, temperature, residual parameters, graph definitions, fold graphs, and stable user assignments. Verify that reloaded candidate scores match in-memory scores before host evaluation. The predictor must receive or preserve the evaluator's user identity to select the exclusion graph. If that interface exposes only sequences, retain its public call signature and use an available stable user-index mapping; if no reliable identity is available, this graph variant is not implementable as specified and must not substitute an ensemble that restores self-support.\n\nDiagnostics: preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions, with view.model referencing the actual trained nn.Module. Retain item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data, and review_data compatibility; do not populate unavailable reviews or item attributes. Add complete scoring that receives sequences, candidates, and an explicit user/fold context. Since the diagnostic loader's first element is None, obtain identity from an explicit diagnostic side channel or directly sample indexed public training histories; never infer identity from sequence contents. Keep encoder-only and complete-score margins separate, record graph-removal effects, embedding norms, cosine alignment, effective rank, and fixed-candidate recent/distant perturbations. Use matched users for perturbation comparisons when both masks change their history, retaining unchanged-history controls. Recompute the residual under perturbed histories while holding the user's graph fixed. If complete scoring lacks user context, return an unavailable metric with a reason rather than mislabeling encoder-only values. Enter evaluation mode for probes and restore the previous mode afterward.\n\nUser diagnosis alignment: 8/10. Positive: explicitly tests recent, specific next-step evidence against broad relevance and older-interest carryover. Negative: no available signal verifies compatibility, quantity needs, duplicate capability, or content tone. Model diagnosis alignment: 8/10. Positive: directly tests candidate discrimination and distinguishes graph improvements from encoder behavior. Negative: stronger training margins, lower alignment, or graph coverage need not improve validation HR@10, and popularity or high-support transitions can become shortcuts.",
