{
  "description": "Add a zero-initialized, strictly causal recent-window residual after each category-specific SASRec’s existing final normalization: z_t = h_t + alpha * r_t. Here r_t is the padding-aware mean of the latest three observed raw item embeddings, including position t, and alpha is a learned category-specific scalar initialized to zero. Keep the existing objective and compare against baseline, last-item-only, and previous-two-only residuals.",
  "motivation": "The hypothesis explicitly targets two user-facing failures: unsupported broad relatedness outranking supported current-purpose matches, and media recommendations drifting across style or mood boundaries. It also targets the observed Electronics/CDs sampled training-pair inversions through the existing next-item supervision. However, the tied-embedding branch adds symmetric similarity rather than identified intent or complementarity, so both user-facing benefits require evidence and could reverse. Missing user panels leave recency dependence unmeasured.",
  "implementation_notes": "Retain all four categories and routing, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, evaluation seed 42, unchanged splits, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], and trial-local artifacts. Honor SASREC_EPOCHS with the supplied environment default of 200; record any override before training. No extra data or pretrained weights are required. Use raw tied item embeddings without positional embeddings or the encoder’s input sqrt(d) multiplier. Add the branch after the existing final LayerNorm, with no new normalization afterward. Thus the extra candidate score is alpha times the average E_history dot E_candidate, a symmetric Gram similarity. Padding-aware denominators replace three when fewer observations are available. The previous-two control uses offsets one and two relative to t; it must return zero when no earlier observations exist. Construct the scalar module after baseline initialization, without additional random draws. Alpha is signed and unconstrained; initialize it exactly to zero and place it in an explicit no-weight-decay optimizer group using the baseline learning rate, betas, and epsilon. Preserve every existing optimizer group and log all resolved settings. Initial equality requires identical baseline weights, finite branch outputs, and identical stochastic state. Alpha can learn immediately; shared embeddings continue receiving baseline encoder/output gradients, although the residual contribution to their gradient starts at zero. Initial equivalence does not guarantee stable later updates. Monitor alpha, branch and baseline norms, abs(alpha)*norm(r)/max(norm(h), epsilon), and candidate-logit differences on a fixed training-only diagnostic sample. Report scale and ranking changes separately. Match baseline and variant checkpoint inspection budgets. Predeclare four attempts including baseline, each capped at 10,800 allocated GPU-seconds with training, diagnostics, debugging, and failures included; enforce the global ceiling across four slots and deduct prior consumption before scheduling. Inspect every 10 completed epochs, use five-inspection patience and at most 20 inspections under the 200-epoch setting, and select by host-returned macro validation HR@10. Ties favor earlier checkpoints and then baseline, last-item, three-item, previous-two. Do not recreate host candidates or fabricate outcomes when unavailable. Store inspected outcomes when exposed by the host; report paired category counts, scores, percentage-point differences, and paired user-bootstrap intervals as exploratory and conditional on fixed models/candidates. Log failures, pruning, overrides, selection opportunities, and actual GPU consumption. Do not claim selection-corrected significance. Write the identical module and configuration into the predictor writer and load alpha in every category checkpoint. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and its metrics/metric_definitions return contract. view.model remains the actual trained nn.Module; view.log2feats returns augmented features, view.item_emb remains the tied table, and predict_score uses those same features. Do not consume the live training iterator merely to obtain diagnostic examples or fabricate missing panels. Use only public histories, timestamps, titles, and descriptions; ratings, reviews, prices, stores, item category labels, and simulator traits remain unavailable. Full-prefix and older-prefix controls, learned lag weights, asymmetric source projections, calibrated gBCE, and adaptive gates remain deferred experiments requiring a declared budget. Shuffling only the uniformly pooled branch cannot test order sensitivity because its output is permutation invariant.",
  "pseudocode": "import torch\nfrom torch import nn\n\nclass RecentResidual(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, window=3, exclude_current=False):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.window = int(window)\n        self.exclude_current = bool(exclude_current)\n        if self.window < 1:\n            raise ValueError('window must be positive')\n        self.alpha = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(()))\n\n    def forward(self, baseline_features, sequence, item_emb):\n        # sequence: B x L, left-padded with zero.\n        # baseline_features: original SASRec output AFTER existing final LN.\n        valid = sequence.ne(0)\n        values = item_emb(sequence)  # raw tied embeddings, no sqrt(d) scaling\n        values = values.masked_fill(~valid.unsqueeze(-1), 0)\n        # Accumulate in float32 for the normal float32/mixed-precision baseline.\n        accum = values.float()\n        sums = torch.cat([\n            accum.new_zeros(accum.size(0), 1, accum.size(2)),\n            accum.cumsum(dim=1)\n        ], dim=1)\n        counts = torch.cat([\n            accum.new_zeros(accum.size(0), 1),\n            valid.to(accum.dtype).cumsum(dim=1)\n        ], dim=1)\n        end = torch.arange(1, sequence.size(1) + 1, device=sequence.device)\n        if self.exclude_current:\n            end = end - 1\n        start = (end - self.window).clamp_min(0)\n        recent_sum = sums[:, end] - sums[:, start]\n        recent_count = counts[:, end] - counts[:, start]\n        recent = recent_sum / recent_count.clamp_min(1).unsqueeze(-1)\n        recent = recent.to(baseline_features.dtype)\n        result = baseline_features + self.alpha * recent\n        return result.masked_fill(~valid.unsqueeze(-1), 0)\n\n# Instantiate AFTER the unchanged baseline modules and their initialization.\n# Primary: RecentResidual(window=3, exclude_current=False).\n# Controls: window=1/current included; window=2/current excluded.\n# Baseline uses the original model without the branch.\n# Add alpha to a separate optimizer group: same baseline lr/betas/eps,\n# weight_decay=0; preserve existing parameter groups.\n# log2feats: original SASRec computation -> existing final LN -> residual.\n# Use augmented features consistently for loss, prediction, and diagnostics.\n# Save window, exclude_current, placement, scaling, optimizer settings, alpha.\n# Predictor writer emits this same definition and category routing.\n# Keep the original next-item objective and all input splits unchanged.\n# Host supplies selection HR@10; no replacement evaluator is constructed.\n# Log each checkpoint inspection and all allocated single-GPU time.\n# If exposed: d_c[u] = int(new_hit_c[u]) - int(baseline_hit_c[u]).\n# delta_macro = mean_c(mean_u(d_c[u])).\n# Bootstrap user indices independently within each category, preserving pairing;\n# compute macro delta for each replicate and report percentile 95% intervals.\n# Label these intervals exploratory, conditional on fixed trained models/candidates.",
  "originality": {
