{
  "description": "Strengthen the existing diagnostic adapter with same-donor, position-preserving replacements that compare recent and older history positions while explicitly measuring matching quality, donor self-recommendation, and dependence on the final item.",
  "motivation": "Current deletions alter content and positions together. Independently selected replacement donors add another confound. Paired replacements using the same donor improve interpretability, while behavioral baselines test whether simple popularity or local associations explain apparent sequence dependence. These diagnostics cannot establish current user purpose, harmful recency bias, or causal lexical dependence.",
  "implementation_notes": "Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and the metrics/metric_definitions return structure. Use view.model as the actual trained module and retain or adapt log2feats, predict_score, item_emb, user_train, meta_data, and review_data. Run in evaluation and inference modes; restore every module's training flag and Python, NumPy, CPU torch, and CUDA RNG states in a finally block. Use an isolated seeded RNG for probes.\n\nBuild statistics exclusively from each dataset's training histories and permitted titles/descriptions. Never consume unavailable ratings, categories, prices, stores, review text, hidden test files, or inferred simulator traits. Standard SASRec consumes IDs and positions: text similarity is a matching variable, not evidence that the model reads words. If examining text-associated effects, cross text similarity with behavioral similarity and report the result as an association with item identity. Do not interpret weak embedding alignment as proof of healthy representations or scale-dependent margins as calibrated ranking accuracy.\n\nPredeclare five draw seeds, 0–4, and select five supplied panel users per category without replacements. For each user, use the original left-padded, truncated sequence. Define the final item as its latest nonpadding position; define earlier recent and older regions by dividing the remaining nonpadding positions into two contiguous halves, with any extra position assigned to the recent half. Skip users without both regions. Sample one position from each region per draw. Never move items, compact padding, or change timestamps. Keeping timestamps attached to positions creates synthetic item/time combinations; document this limitation. Final-position edits form a separate analysis and are not pooled with earlier-position contrasts.\n\nDefine item frequency f(j) from training interactions, P(j)=f(j)/sum_k f(k), adjacent transition counts n(i,j), and n(i)=sum_j n(i,j). Use P(j|i)=[n(i,j)+alpha*P(j)]/[n(i)+alpha] with alpha=10 fixed before inspecting diagnostic outcomes; retain raw counts as support indicators. Fit optional word TF-IDF on training-catalog titles/descriptions using lowercase unigrams/bigrams, min_df=2, max_features=50000, and L2 normalization. Missing text is missing, not zero semantic similarity.\n\nRequire a common donor outside padding and the user's complete original history. For both displaced items, require absolute log-popularity distance at most 0.5 training-catalog SD and absolute log-smoothed incoming-transition-probability distance at most 0.5 SD, using the same predecessor at each edited position; handle a missing predecessor as a separate stratum. Require the two donor-to-displaced-item TF-IDF cosines to differ by at most 0.1 when both are available. These are proposed operational defaults, not thresholds justified by Austin's propensity-score results. Choose uniformly among feasible donors using the draw RNG. Skip pairs without joint support rather than substituting different donors or relaxing calipers after inspecting effects. Report chosen positions, IDs, match distances, standardized balance differences where estimable, raw transition support, text availability, unmatched fractions, and donor reuse. Sparse overlap may substantially restrict conclusions.\n\nFor each user, keep C equal to the full catalog excluding the complete original training history across all edits. Also define C_minus by excluding every donor selected for that user's recorded edits from C. Recompute the unedited baseline separately on each pool; compare each edit only with its corresponding baseline. This prevents newly inserted or removed items from changing eligibility and provides a common donor-excluded audit. Score in chunks on one GPU, with deterministic item-ID tie breaking. Report top-10 overlap, mean absolute rank displacement of original top-10 items, strict pair-order reversals, absolute logit change, final-feature L2 change, donor score/rank movement on C, and recent-minus-older paired differences. Identity replacements and repeated inference must reproduce baseline scores and rankings within documented numerical tolerance. Preserve existing positive-rescaling checks where applicable.\n\nAverage draws within each user before equal category averaging and the four-category macro average. Report individual effects and leave-one-user-out sensitivity within categories. Optional percentile intervals use 2000 within-category user bootstrap draws conditional on fixed recorded matches, with seed 42. Label them exploratory: five users/category provide weak uncertainty estimates, repeated donors are not independent users, and these intervals exclude rematching, training-seed variability, and adaptive search. Do not bootstrap and rematch fixed-neighbor estimators without a justified procedure.\n\nKeep the candidate-set disagreement audit and evidence-backed unresolved-case rubric as separate directions. Add low-cost training-only popularity, smoothed last-item transition, and recency-weighted pairwise scoring controls before expensive new architectures. An illustrative pairwise score is sum_t w_t*P(j|i_t), with normalized weights w_t proportional to 2^[-age(t)/10]; freeze this default before evaluation. Prefix shuffling with the last item fixed and last-item-only inference provide complementary controls but can introduce distribution shift. Their apparent success or low model sensitivity cannot prove the absence of higher-order learning.\n\nDo not edit benchmark inputs or splits. Evaluate shortlisted trained interventions through the unchanged host evaluator using preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, evaluation seed 42, 200 validation users/category, and 99 unique negatives. Report per-category and equal-category macro HR@10, including paired changes only when the host exposes the necessary unchanged user-level outcomes. Training-only audit holdouts, if used, are auxiliary and must never replace or modify benchmark splits. No diagnostic becomes a surrogate selection objective. Avoid selecting calipers or interventions repeatedly on the twenty-user panel; log hypotheses and diagnostic cost, and cap donor search/scoring to respect the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. No training or improvement is claimed in this report.\n\nRetain all four routes, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], single-GPU execution, and artifacts beneath the trial directory. Respect SASREC_EPOCHS=200 when provided by the environment. Diagnostic-only changes do not require architecture changes; any later architecture or inference change must update the predictor writer so working/predict.py loads the trained model consistently.",
  "pseudocode": "run_full_diagnosis(train_loader):\n    save all module flags and global RNG states\n    try:\n        enter evaluation and inference modes\n        build training-only frequency, transition, optional TF-IDF statistics\n        for each dataset and its five supplied panel users:\n            x = original padded training sequence\n            C = catalog minus complete original training history\n            plan = []\n            for seed in [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]:\n                choose one older and one recent nonfinal position\n                find donors jointly satisfying declared calipers at both positions\n                if no shared donor exists:\n                    record unmatched pair; continue\n                sample one shared donor using isolated seeded RNG\n                record two separate replacements, match residuals, and support\n            plan final-position edits separately\n            C_minus = C minus all donors recorded for this user\n            for pool in [C, C_minus]:\n                baseline = score(x, pool, deterministic_ties=True)\n                verify identity replacement and repeated-inference audits\n                for planned paired edit:\n                    score recent and older replacement separately on pool\n                    record paired stability and original-top-10 rank changes\n                score final-position edits as a separate stratum\n            record donor score/rank changes on C\n            average paired draws within this user\n        aggregate equally within categories, then across four categories\n        report individual effects, coverage, balance, failures, and cost\n        report leave-one-user-out sensitivity\n        optionally bootstrap users within categories with matches fixed\n        label sensitivity, uncertainty limits, and missing evidence explicitly\n        return metrics, metric_definitions\n    finally:\n        restore all module flags and global RNG states\n\nRun cheap training-only behavioral controls as separate baselines.\nSelect subsequent trained interventions only with unchanged host macro validation HR@10.",
