      - Analysis: {"status":"NEEDS_IMPROVEMENT","core_findings":["Training-pair ordering is substantially weaker in Electronics and CDs_and_Vinyl: negatives beat positives in 22.1% and 25.9% of sampled positions, versus 3.1% for Movies_and_TV and 1.7% for Industrial_and_Scientific. Raw margin magnitudes depend on logit scale and do not establish validation accuracy.","Embedding geometry shows no broad collapse: effective-rank fractions are 0.988–0.996, with low mean pairwise cosine. Missing user panels and validation outcomes prevent conclusions about history sensitivity or benchmark HR@10."],"key_implications":["Electronics and CDs_and_Vinyl discriminate observed positives from sampled unseen negatives less consistently under the training-only probe.","Broad embedding dimensionality is preserved despite the category differences in training-pair ordering."],"evidence":{"headline_metrics":{"negative_beats_positive_rate":{"Movies_and_TV":0.0312,"Industrial_and_Scientific":0.0171,"Electronics":0.2206,"CDs_and_Vinyl":0.2591},"effective_rank_fraction_range":[0.9879,0.9956]},"brief_metric_read":{"negative_beats_positive_rate":"Training-only ordering errors; not validation accuracy.","effective_rank_fraction_range":"High sampled dimensionality; does not establish user diversity."}}}
    
