Reflection 1: current report is not sufficient because ["The report understates 
SASRec's directional capacity: causal sequence encoding already permits 
asymmetric transition scores. The residual adds a particular bilinear transition
path; it does not introduce directionality to an otherwise symmetric model.", 
'The proposed symmetric control is confounded. Separate projections yield M=AᵀB,
whereas a shared projection yields M=AᵀA, which is positive semidefinite before 
scalar scaling. Differences therefore reflect expressiveness and 
parameterization as well as asymmetry. Measure the antisymmetric component and 
consider a symmetric-indefinite control.', 'The objective is not reproducible: 
negative count, replacement policy, eligibility exclusions, temperature, loss 
reduction, and projection optimizer settings are unspecified. Keeping CE across 
architecture arms isolates architecture, but comparing against the original BCE 
baseline also changes the objective; include a plain SASRec-with-CE control.', 
'Remaining budget, per-arm allocations, checkpoint inspection cadence, stopping 
rules, and tie-breaking are unspecified. Historical combined_score values are 
not established as comparable pinned macro validation HR@10 results.', 'The 
description initially presents augmented feature and candidate vectors whose dot
product introduces unwanted cross terms; only the later additive score and 
concatenated implementation are consistent. Use one authoritative equation 
throughout.', 'Originality is incremental given established asymmetric 
sequential factorization methods. Difficulty 3 appears optimistic because 
candidate-axis broadcasting, diagnostic adaptation, predictor generation, and 
budget orchestration remain unfinished. Potential 7 is a hypothesis unsupported 
by matched validation evidence.', 'Recent-item bilinear scoring can reinforce 
popularity, persistent affinity, and related-variant shortcuts. Unavailable 
perturbation results provide no evidence that excessive older-history influence 
causes the observed errors.'], generating follow-up queries
