INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: At 10% progress, prioritize a small **directional recent-history residual**, keeping candidate-set cross-entropy unchanged. Earlier residual attempts scored 0.4037–0.4100 versus the supplied SASRec reference of 0.5050; these results discourage assuming that additional pooling improves validation.

**Insights and directions.** First, the starting idea separates recent evidence from the backbone. Zero initialization permits an initially identical scoring function, but does not guarantee useful subsequent learning. Second, recurrence weighting may emphasize repeated interests. It can also suppress a new interest, so defer it until simpler mechanisms succeed. Third, candidate-set CE makes negative competition explicit. Electronics’ 15.96% sampled negative-win rate motivates investigating separation, without establishing its cause.

From related work, SASRec provides causal context selection. It does not explicitly distinguish directional transitions from symmetric affinity [1]. TiCoSeRec motivates ordered behavioral transitions. A lightweight asymmetric residual tests that principle without constructing its graph [2]. gSASRec motivates objective calibration and additional negatives. Its evidence does not establish superiority over the proposed CE objective [3].

These insights organize three directions: context selection, directional scoring, and objective calibration.

| Framework axis | Existing mechanism | Proposed experiment | Remaining gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| Context | SASRec; recent pooling | Three-event exponential pooling | Recent does not establish current purpose |
| Relations | Tied embedding similarity | Separate source/target projections | Transitions do not establish compatibility |
| Competition | Candidate-set CE; gBCE | Keep CE fixed initially | Sampled margins differ from validation HR@10 |

**Candidate assessment.** Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Each entry gives a positive and negative reason.

| Idea | Scores | Positive / negative assessment by dimension |
|---|---|---|
| A. Directional recent residual | 4/7/7/6/3 | O: specific combination / established components; P: extensible / sparse transitions; U: recent successors / usefulness unverified; M: supervised separation / cause uncertain; D: small projections / serialization care |
| B. Recency–recurrence gate | 3/6/7/5/5 | O: causal support / familiar gating; P: adaptive evidence / interest lock-in; U: narrow recurrence / novel interests suppressed; M: context filtering / sensitivity unmeasured; D: backbone retained / masking complexity |
| C. Matched-negative gBCE | 2/6/3/8/3 | O: controlled transfer / established loss; P: calibration / CE advantage unknown; U: fewer distractors / no intent mechanism; M: direct competition / sampled proxy; D: local change / sampling cost |

**Selected: A.** It explicitly targets broad relatedness displacing recent narrow interests and persistent demand assumptions that promote related variants. Ordered successor supervision may distinguish useful next choices from symmetric affinity; both benefits remain hypotheses. Its model-facing target is sampled positive–negative inversion, especially in Electronics.

Pool only causal recent embeddings; score through separate source and target projections, with a zero-start residual coefficient. Compare against unchanged SASRec and an otherwise matched symmetric residual before adding gates or changing losses. Select exclusively by pinned four-category macro validation HR@10; preserve all routing, splits, seeds, interfaces, and budget accounting. No new gain is claimed.

References: [1] https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.09781; [2] https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01056; [3] https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: At 10% progress, prioritize a small **directional recent-history residual**, conditional on establishing a matched objective baseline. Earlier residual attempts scored 0.4037–0.4100 versus the supplied SASRec reference of 0.5050; without matched configurations, these results discourage optimism but do not identify the cause.

**Insights and directions.** First, zero initialization preserves initial scores, not subsequent optimization. This supports controlled residual experiments. Second, recurrence can emphasize narrow interests but suppress emerging ones. Defer recurrence gating. Third, Electronics’ 15.96% sampled negative-win rate motivates separation experiments without identifying their cause.

Related work supplies three further insights. SASRec already produces directional, history-dependent predictions despite tied embeddings [1]. FPMC/FOSSIL establish asymmetric transitions and pooled recent histories, reducing novelty [2]. gSASRec motivates negative-count/calibration studies, while candidate CE is a separate intervention [3,4]. These organize context selection, relation parameterization, and objective calibration.

| Framework axis | Existing mechanism | Proposed experiment | Remaining gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| Context | SASRec; FOSSIL history pooling | Three-event exponential pooling | Recency does not establish purpose |
| Relations | FPMC/FOSSIL directed factors | Zero-start bilinear residual | Direction does not establish compatibility |
| Competition | Original BCE; multi-negative BCE; CE | Matched sampler/objective controls | Sampled margins differ from validation HR@10 |

**Candidate assessment.** Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Each dimension has a positive and negative reason.

| Idea | Scores | Positive / negative assessment by dimension |
|---|---|---|
| A. Directional recent residual | 2/6/6/6/4 | O: specific SASRec extension / strong prior art; P: extensible / sparse transitions; U: recent successors / usefulness unverified; M: separation / cause uncertain; D: small module / matched controls |
| B. Recency–recurrence gate | 3/6/7/5/5 | O: causal support / familiar gating; P: adaptive evidence / interest lock-in; U: narrow recurrence / new interests suppressed; M: context filtering / sensitivity unmeasured; D: backbone retained / masking complexity |
| C. Matched-negative gBCE | 2/6/3/8/3 | O: controlled transfer / established loss; P: calibration / CE advantage unknown; U: fewer distractors / no intent mechanism; M: competition / sampled proxy; D: local change / sampling cost |

**Selected: A.** Explicit user-facing targets are broad relatedness displacing recent narrow interests and past artist/device-family interactions implying continuing demand for related variants. Recent ordered-successor supervision tests both hypotheses; it cannot identify satisfied demand, compatibility, or replacement need. Its model-facing target is sampled positive–negative inversion, especially Electronics.

Use causal pooling and separate source/target projections with a zero-start coefficient. Establish original BCE, matched multi-negative BCE, and matched CE references before attributing gains to architecture. Compare full bilinear and parameter-matched symmetric residuals; defer additional gates. Training-only skew-score and popularity-matched transition probes assess shortcuts, not relevance. Preserve routing, splits, seeds, interfaces, and shared budget; select only by pinned macro validation HR@10. Repeated validation selection remains exploratory. No gain is claimed.

References: [1] [SASRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.09781); [2] [FOSSIL/FPMC](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09152); [3] [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf); [4] [CE implementation](https://github.com/antklen/sasrec-bert4rec-recsys23).
