INFO:self_evolverec:The new idea for model in iteration 4:
{
  "description": "Add a zero-initialized directional recent-history scoring residual to each category-specific RecHarness SASRec: score(t,i) = h_t^T E_i + alpha (A r_t)^T(B E_i), where r_t pools the latest three causal raw item embeddings with weights 1, 0.5, 0.25, excluding padding and renormalizing. A and B are independent, bias-free d-by-d projections initialized to identity; alpha starts at zero. Do not implement this by multiplying augmented backbone and candidate vectors, which introduces unintended cross terms. SASRec already models directed predictions; the hypothesis concerns the incremental benefit of an explicit recent bilinear path. Candidate-set CE is an experimental objective, not the original RecHarness SASRec loss. Establish matched objective references before assessing this architecture.",
  "motivation": "The two explicit user-facing targets are broad relatedness crowding out recent narrow interests and past interactions with an artist, device family, or product type being interpreted as continuing demand for related variants. Recency weighting emphasizes immediate evidence; ordered successor supervision may downrank related variants that do not commonly follow that evidence. Both are hypotheses, and the model cannot identify actual ownership, fulfilled demand, replacement need, compatibility, dimensions, quantity, or mood. The model-facing target is positive–negative ranking inversion: Electronics has a 0.1596 sampled negative-win rate and a −0.4829 tenth-percentile margin; Industrial_and_Scientific also has a negative lower-tail margin. Sampled margins depend on the sampler and score scale and do not establish validation quality. Earlier residual scores below the supplied reference motivate stronger controls rather than a claim that residuals or asymmetry will help.",
  "implementation_notes": "Preserve four independently trained category models and routing for Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics, and CDs_and_Vinyl. Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, unchanged input splits, trial-local artifacts, single-GPU execution, and SASREC_EPOCHS with the supplied environment default of 200. Use only public training histories, timestamps, titles, and descriptions. This experiment requires histories alone. Reviews, ratings, item category labels, prices, stores, ownership, and simulator diversity/conformity traits remain unavailable; generic example schemas do not authorize their use.\n\nArchitecture: retain baseline dimensions, encoder, final normalization, masking, and raw tied item embeddings. Pool positions t, t−1, t−2 with weights 1, 0.5, 0.25 and padding-aware renormalization. No positional embeddings or encoder sqrt(d) multiplier enter this branch. Pooling uses positions within the original sequence, without consulting later items. Return zero for empty windows and padded queries. Add no normalization after the score residual. Construct baseline modules and perform their initialization first. Construct A and B afterward inside a separate RNG context that restores CPU and applicable CUDA RNG states; initialize both to identity and alpha exactly to zero. Initial equality requires identical baseline weights, finite branch outputs, and matched stochastic state. A and B receive zero residual gradients initially; alpha can learn immediately. Once alpha changes, independent A and B can learn asymmetry. Zero initialization does not ensure later stability.\n\nOptimizer: preserve every baseline parameter group and resolved optimizer setting. Add A and B in an explicit group using the baseline learning rate, betas, epsilon, and the existing policy for comparable projection weights; log its weight decay. Put alpha in a separate group with the same optimizer settings and zero weight decay. Keep update counts, batch size, encoder dimensions, regularization, validation opportunities, and checkpoint rules matched across comparisons. Explicitly document any mismatch forced by the GPU budget. Preserving RNG state prevents branch construction from changing initial training randomness; it does not make later stochastic trajectories identical across different objectives.\n\nObjective/sampler contract for new multi-negative arms: K=256, temperature=1.0. At each valid timestep independently sample a uniform subset without replacement from the category catalog excluding padding and that user's COMPLETE training-positive set. Never consult held-out labels. Resample on each presentation; do not share sampled sets across timesteps. If fewer than K items are eligible, use all eligible items and mask unused slots. Log eligible-pool shortages; this contract requires user identity or a precomputed per-example exclusion handle internally. Preserve the diagnostic loader's (None, sequence, positive, negative) interface through a separate adapter; do not attempt to reconstruct complete user histories from truncated sequences. Rows with no eligible negatives contribute zero to the specified objective and should be counted separately. Skip batches with no valid targets. Use chunked scoring if needed; do not allocate a full B×L×K×d tensor. Candidate CE is -s_positive + logsumexp([s_positive, eligible negative scores]), summed over positive!=0 positions and divided by their count. Do not divide CE by K or average over padded B×L positions. Multi-negative BCE is softplus(-s_positive) + mean_j softplus(s_negative_j), averaged over valid targets; define the zero-negative row contribution as zero to match its uninformative candidate CE counterpart and report its frequency. These are proposed reproducibility choices, not SASRec/gSASRec defaults. Original reference training remains unchanged, including its own sampler and normalization.\n\nExperimental sequence: R0 unchanged RecHarness SASRec+BCE; R1 plain SASRec+multi-negative BCE with the specified sampler; R2 plain SASRec+candidate CE with that same sampler; R3 full residual+CE. R0 versus R1 measures the combined negative-count/sampler change, not negative count alone if eligibility differs. R1 versus R2 isolates the defined objective change. R2 versus R3 isolates the residual under CE. Residual+BCE completes the interaction check if budget permits; do not claim objective-independent gains without it. Match updates and validation opportunities while reporting actual GPU consumption, since equal updates and equal runtime are different comparisons. Run arms sequentially in priority order within the externally supplied remaining budget; these arms are not an instruction to exceed it.\n\nAsymmetry control: for column-vector embeddings, M=A^T B and residual=r^T M e. The previous shared-projection control A^T A is positive semidefinite and halves projection parameters, confounding symmetry with capacity and spectral constraints. Instead retrain a control with score alpha/2*((A r)^T(B e)+(B r)^T(A e)), using the same A,B dimensions, identity initialization, parameter count, optimizer policy, and validation opportunities. This implements S=(M+M^T)/2. Report effective matrix ranks: symmetric controls can have different rank capacity for low-rank factors, although d-by-d factors here both have rank at most d. At full rank this symmetric parameterization permits indefinite matrices. Skew-only retraining and a parameter-matched signed-Gram control C^T J C are deferred, budget-dependent extensions. Supplement retraining by removing K=(M−M^T)/2 from the trained full model; this ablation is descriptive and does not replace a retrained control. Directional novelty must be assessed against FPMC/FOSSIL, not just tied similarity. TiCoSeRec supplies additional directed-transition motivation, not evidence for this specific residual.\n\nDiagnostics: preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions. view.model must be the actual trained nn.Module; view.item_emb remains the shared E table. log2feats returns concatenated scoring features, and predict_score uses matching candidate representations. Adapt every scoring probe to call predict_score rather than assuming feature and embedding dimensions coincide. Preserve user_train, meta_data, and review_data interfaces without fabricating missing fields. Keep train_loader outputs (None, sequence, positive, negative), each tensor B×L. Use a separate fixed diagnostic sample or loader; do not consume or perturb the live training iterator. Preserve existing sampled-margin definitions and the embedding-alignment definition; alignment measures E only and is not proof of collapse. Supplementary probes require explicit shared remaining-budget accounting; unavailable probes remain unavailable.\n\nOn a fixed training-only sample, monitor alpha, feature norms, absolute residual/base score magnitudes, candidate-score differences, and unchanged sampled negative-win rates. For M=S+K, report actual |alpha*r^T K e|, symmetric contributions, and total residual contributions; parameter norms alone do not establish useful asymmetry. For single-item sources, compare forward-minus-reverse scores, excluding biases, with training transition-count asymmetry. Compare genuine transitions with reversed pairs and frequency-matched nontransitions using identical fixed candidates; stratify by source/target frequency and transition support. These diagnose popularity and first-order shortcuts, not higher-order reasoning or practical usefulness. Optional perturbations include earlier-item shuffling preserving the last item, swapping the last two, dropping recent items, and frequency/similarity-matched last-item replacement. Pooling is order-sensitive only through unequal lag weights; do not claim that any shuffle necessarily changes its output. Perturbed histories may be implausible. Popularity-only, last-item similarity, and first-order transition baselines are descriptive training probes, not additional selection metrics.\n\nFor the twenty designated real training users, retain full-catalog unseen-item recommendations, five users per category, excluding all observed training items. Separately report coverage, recommendation popularity, concentration, and recent-history similarity when budget permits. These statistics do not establish relevance, user conformity, or simulator preference traits. Sampled validation and full-catalog diagnostics can disagree; only the pinned host macro validation HR@10 selects models.\n\nBudget/selection: subtract all previous consumption from the shared 43,200 GPU-second allowance before scheduling. The host must charge actual single-GPU time for training, checkpoint inspections, diagnostics, debugging, and failures across all slots; local elapsed-time limits alone cannot enforce the shared balance. Preserve and record the baseline validation cadence and checkpoint policy. Compare category models at the same declared configuration/checkpoint opportunity and select their bundle by host-provided four-category macro validation HR@10; do not independently choose category checkpoints unless a separately declared validation search permits and accounts for that additional flexibility. Ties favor earlier checkpoints, then the simpler configuration. Do not recreate or modify host candidates, access hidden tests, or fabricate unavailable outcomes. Retain per-category scores and all attempted configurations when exposed. Optional paired user-bootstrap intervals require host-provided paired hits and describe uncertainty conditional on fixed models/candidates, not seed stability or selection-corrected significance. Repeated validation inspection can overfit 800 fixed users, so restrict the declared search and treat gains as exploratory. Final held-out inference remains the separate host step.\n\nSerialization: update the predictor writer with identical architecture, pooling weights, scoring mode, dimensions, alpha, A/B parameters, mappings, and category routing. Store resolved sampler/objective settings and optimizer configuration with each trial. Before training, verify finite empty-window handling, zero-alpha valid-position baseline-score equality, suffix invariance of earlier scores, a finite nonzero alpha gradient on a nondegenerate example, and checkpoint-reload score equality. Test all supported candidate layouts explicitly. No such verification or training has been executed in this report. Potential shortcut learning includes head-item preference, local co-occurrence, symmetric similarity, and repeated-interest lock-in; the proposed controls constrain the claims rather than eliminating these risks.",
  "pseudocode": "import torch\nfrom torch import nn\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\nclass DirectionalRecentResidual(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, dim, symmetric=False):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.source = nn.Linear(dim, dim, bias=False)\n        self.target = nn.Linear(dim, dim, bias=False)\n        nn.init.eye_(self.source.weight)\n        nn.init.eye_(self.target.weight)\n        self.alpha = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(()))\n        self.symmetric = bool(symmetric)\n\n    def features(self, baseline_features, sequence, item_emb):\n        valid = sequence.ne(0)\n        emb = item_emb(sequence).masked_fill(~valid.unsqueeze(-1), 0)\n        # Float32 pooling avoids small-denominator underflow with mixed precision.\n        values = emb.float()\n        pooled = torch.zeros_like(values)\n        count = values.new_zeros(sequence.shape)\n        for lag, weight in enumerate((1.0, 0.5, 0.25)):\n            if lag >= sequence.size(1):\n                break\n            if lag == 0:\n                shifted_emb, shifted_valid = values, valid\n            else:\n                shifted_emb = F.pad(values[:, :-lag], (0, 0, lag, 0))\n                shifted_valid = F.pad(valid[:, :-lag], (lag, 0))\n            pooled = pooled + weight * shifted_emb\n            count = count + weight * shifted_valid.float()\n        pooled = (pooled / count.clamp_min(1e-8).unsqueeze(-1)).to(emb.dtype)\n        if self.symmetric:\n            parts = (baseline_features,\n                     0.5 * self.alpha * self.source(pooled),\n                     0.5 * self.alpha * self.target(pooled))\n        else:\n            parts = (baseline_features, self.alpha * self.source(pooled))\n        return torch.cat(parts, dim=-1).masked_fill(~valid.unsqueeze(-1), 0)\n\n    def candidates(self, item_ids, item_emb):\n        e = item_emb(item_ids)\n        if self.symmetric:\n            return torch.cat((e, self.target(e), self.source(e)), dim=-1)\n        return torch.cat((e, self.target(e)), dim=-1)\n\n    def score_aligned(self, features, item_ids, item_emb):\n        # features: (..., D); item_ids: matching (...).\n        return (features * self.candidates(item_ids, item_emb)).sum(-1)\n\n    def score_per_position(self, features, item_ids, item_emb):\n        # features: (..., D); item_ids: (..., K).\n        return (features.unsqueeze(-2) * self.candidates(item_ids, item_emb)).sum(-1)\n\n    def score_shared_catalog(self, features, item_ids, item_emb):\n        # features: (..., D); item_ids: (C,). Call with catalog chunks.\n        return features @ self.candidates(item_ids, item_emb).transpose(-1, -2)\n\n# Construct baseline and complete its unchanged initialization first.\n# Create the residual within torch.random.fork_rng(devices=applicable_cuda_indices)\n# so nn.Linear initialization does not advance the baseline training RNG stream.\n# The actual SASRec.log2feats returns residual.features(original_h, sequence, item_emb).\n# Actual model.predict_score dispatches EXPLICITLY by documented candidate layout;\n# never infer ambiguous layouts solely from tensor rank.\n\n# Multi-negative training contract; host data pipeline supplies complete training\n# exclusion sets through internal user handles, independent of diagnostic tuples.\nfor batch in training_batches:\n    sequence, positive, user_handles = batch\n    valid = positive.ne(0)\n    if not valid.any():\n        continue\n    features = model.log2feats(sequence)[valid]\n    targets = positive[valid]\n    # Independently resample each row, uniformly WITHOUT replacement.\n    # Exclude 0 and COMPLETE training positives; use all eligible if fewer than 256.\n    negatives, neg_mask = sample_uniform_training_complement(\n        user_handles, valid, K=256, share_across_timesteps=False)\n    pos_score = model.predict_score(features, targets, layout='aligned')\n    neg_score = score_negative_chunks(model, features, negatives)\n    has_negative = neg_mask.any(-1)\n    if objective == 'candidate_ce':\n        masked_neg = neg_score.masked_fill(~neg_mask, -torch.inf)\n        logits = torch.cat((pos_score[:, None], masked_neg), dim=-1) / 1.0\n        per_row = F.cross_entropy(\n            logits, torch.zeros_like(targets, dtype=torch.long), reduction='none')\n    elif objective == 'multi_negative_bce':\n        neg_term = F.softplus(neg_score).masked_fill(~neg_mask, 0).sum(-1)\n        neg_term = neg_term / neg_mask.sum(-1).clamp_min(1)\n        per_row = F.softplus(-pos_score) + neg_term\n        per_row = per_row.masked_fill(~has_negative, 0)\n    loss = per_row.sum() / valid.sum()\n    # Count zero-negative rows; skip optimizer update if every row has zero loss.\n    if has_negative.any():\n        optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\n        loss.backward()\n        optimizer.step()\n\n# R0 uses the UNCHANGED RecHarness baseline training path, not this new sampler.\n# R1: plain + specified multi-negative BCE. R2: plain + specified CE.\n# R3: full residual + CE. Next: parameter-matched symmetric residual + CE.\n# Residual+BCE and skew-only retraining are conditional on remaining shared budget.\n# Serialize scoring mode and all parameters into checkpoints and predictor writer.\n# Select bundles solely by host-provided four-category macro validation HR@10.",
