tervention will help.\n\nBefore new outcomes, publish a versioned configuration manifest defining all baseline hyperparameters, action configurations, diagnostic cutoffs, missing-data behavior, action-priority mappings, tie rules and stopping conditions. A concrete minimal residual configuration is k=5 causal nonpadding items ending at position t, including its current input item, with no future positions; r_t is their masked mean item embedding. Define h'_t = h_t + sigmoid(b) * LayerNorm(r_t), with an independent learned scalar b initialized to -4, LayerNorm scale 1 and bias 0. Use h'_t consistently for training and inference, with padding masked. This is a proposed implementation choice, not a published HGN or SLi-Rec architecture. If expanded, preregister last-item-only, masked recent mean, fixed residual and learned-gate ablations rather than adding them after outcomes.\n\nFor the sampling action, fix rho=0.25 and preserve the baseline's negative count. Build directed adjacent-transition counts from training interactions only. At a training position ending with item i, the hard pool contains observed successors of i excluding padding, the positive target and all items in that user's complete training history. Draw uniformly from the hard pool with probability rho and otherwise uniformly from the eligible catalog; fall back to uniform when the hard pool is empty. Skip and count positions with no eligible negatives. This mixture is an implementation choice, not GNNO. Transition candidates can be false negatives, so report pool coverage and target-popularity distributions and avoid assuming harder sampling improves HR@10. Keep gSASRec/gBCE as a separately declared alternative rather than silently changing negative counts or loss within this comparison.\n\nUse a deliberately simple frozen policy: for each route, if the independently sampled training-negative outranking rate exceeds 0.15, prioritize the sampling action; otherwise prioritize the residual. These cutoffs are operational hypotheses, not calibrated thresholds. Order remaining actions deterministically and abstain to the common baseline if required diagnostics fail. Avoid using the twenty-user panel to tune this mapping. A future learned mapping requires separate tasks or complete trajectories for fitting and evaluation; four routes do not support credible cross-fitted policy-learning claims.\n\nAll arms start from the same prespecified strong SASRec incumbent and share the identical configuration set. Its training/evaluation cost must be charged equally as a common initialization charge and counted once in the global physical-cost ledger. Diagnostic guidance changes order only. Random search draws configurations uniformly without replacement from the same manifest. Match worker count, initialization, evaluation opportunities and stopping rules. A placebo arm computes the same diagnostics and prospectively permutes complete route-level guidance bundles, preserving repeated actions and temporal blocks. There are at most 24 route permutations, potentially fewer distinct assignments. Record the randomization mechanism, action frequencies and realized action changes. Identical guidance makes the placebo uninformative. Without route exchangeability, interpret this as a randomized policy perturbation or sensitivity analysis, not an exact permutation significance test.\n\nPartition the remaining shared 43,200 GPU-second budget before execution. Equal arm budgets include initialization charges, diagnostics, training, validation, debugging, failures and consumed resources of unfinished trials. Sum consumption across concurrent single-GPU trials; report elapsed wall time and CPU/LLM overhead separately. Use synchronized elapsed allocation time for each active GPU worker, rather than kernel-active time. Do not launch a trial unless its declared allocation fits the remaining allowance; stop at the deadline regardless of estimated completion. Only eligible host evaluations completed before cutoff update the incumbent; carry the previous incumbent forward and never backfill late results. Unfinished final HR@10 is missing, not a censored score bound. Record complete optimization traces and endpoint scores; if reporting area under the incumbent curve, use a fixed common budget horizon and a step-function incumbent beginning at the common baseline.\n\nRepeat whole adaptive searches with paired search seeds only if the shared budget permits complete matched allocations; do not manufacture replication by treating categories or repeated actions as independent searches. Keep preprocessing/training/evaluation seeds at 0/42/42. Search-seed repetitions estimate search randomness, not training-seed robustness, and do not remove adaptive validation bias. Freeze methods and final selection before the host's held-out evaluation. Host macro validation HR@10 alone selects models; report per-category outcomes, coverage, completed trials, all costs and exploratory paired differences. Do not create an additional validation evaluator or modify pinned candidate sets.\n\nPreserve original splits, all four dataset routes, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts, single-GPU execution per trial and the environment's SASREC_EPOCHS default of 200. Update the predictor writer and compatibility adapter for every architecture or scoring change so working/predict.py reconstructs exactly the trained model. No training, testing, filesystem inspection or benchmark improvement is claimed in this report.",
